window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2016-12-1", "source": "2016年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on invention. Your essay should include the importance of invention and measures to be taken to encourage invention. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Invention has long been a driving force behind human civilization. In my view, we should fully recognize its\nsignificance and take concrete steps to encourage more of it.\nTo begin with, invention is of great importance. It pushes social progress by turning bold ideas into practical\ntechnologies that reshape how we live and work. The smartphone, for example, has transformed\ncommunication, education and business within a single generation. Moreover, a nation rich in inventions\nenjoys stronger competitiveness and a firmer place in the global economy.\nGiven these benefits, effective measures should be taken. First, the government ought to increase funding for\nscientific research so that inventors have the resources they need. In addition, a sound system to protect\nintellectual property is essential, for it assures creators that their efforts will be rewarded. Finally, schools\nshould foster curiosity and creativity from an early age.\nIn conclusion, invention deserves our attention, and only through steady support and a genuinely innovative\natmosphere can we keep the flow of new ideas alive."}, "translation": {"prompt": "随着生活水平的提高，度假在中国人生活中的作用越来越重要。过去，中国人的时间主要花在谋生上，很少有机会外出旅行。然而，近年来中国旅游业发展迅速。经济的繁荣和富裕中产阶级的出现，引发了一个前所未有的旅游热潮。中国人不仅在国内旅游，出国旅游也越来越普遍。2016年国庆节假日期间，旅游消费总计超过4000亿元。据世界贸易组织估计，2020年中国将成为世界上最大的旅游国，在未来几年里将成为出境旅游支出增长最快的国家。", "sample": "With the improvement of living standards, vacationing plays an increasingly important role in Chinese people's life. In the past, Chinese people spent most of their time making a living and had few chances to travel. However, China's tourism has developed rapidly in recent years. Economic prosperity and the emergence of an affluent middle class have given rise to an unprecedented tourism boom. Chinese people not only travel within China, but traveling abroad is also becoming increasingly common. During the National Day holiday in 2016, tourism spending totaled more than 400 billion yuan. According to estimates by the World Trade Organization, China will become the world's largest tourism country by 2020, and in the coming years it will become the country with the fastest-growing outbound tourism spending."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2016-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "It is important that scientists be seen as normal people asking and answering important questions. Good, sound science depends on  __26__  , experiments and reasoned methodologies. It requires a willingness to ask new questions and try new approaches. It requires one to take risks and experience failures. But good science also requires  __27__  understanding, clear explanation and concise presentation.\nOur country needs more scientists who are willing to step out in the public  __28__  and offer their opinions on important matters. We need more scientists who can explain what they are doing in language that is  __29__  and understandable to the public. Those of us who are not scientists should also be prepared to support public engagement by scientists, and to  __30__  scientific knowledge into our public communications.\nToo many people in this country, including some among our elected leadership, still do not understand how science works or why robust, long-range investments in research vitally matter. In the 1960s, the United States  __31__  nearly 17% of discretionary(可酌情支配的) spending to research and development,  __32__  decades of economic growth. By 2008, the figure had fallen into the single  __33__  . This occurs at a time when other nations have made significant gains in their own research capabilities.\nAt the University of California (UC), we  __34__  ourselves not only on the quality of our research, but also on its contribution to improving our world. To  __35__  the development of science from the lab bench to the market place, UC is investing our own money in our own good ideas.", "matchIntro": "Are We in an Innovation Lull?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Scan the highlights of this year's Consumer Electronics Show (CES), and you may get a slight feeling of having seen them before. Many of the coolest gadgets this year are the same as the coolest gadgets last year—or the year before, even. The booths are still exciting, and the demos are still just as crazy. It is still easy to be dazzled by the display of drones(无人机), 3D printers, virtual reality goggles(眼镜) and more \"smart\" devices than you could ever hope to catalog. Upon reflection, however, it is equally easy to feel like you have seen it all before. And it is hard not to think: Are we in an innovation lull(间歇期)?"}, {"label": "B", "text": "In some ways, the answer is yes. For years, smartphones, televisions, tablets, laptops and desktops have made up a huge part of the market and driven innovation. But now these segments are looking at slower growth curves—or shrinking markets in some cases—as consumers are not as eager to spend money on new gadgets. Meanwhile, emerging technologies—the drones, 3D printers and smart-home devices of the world—now seem a bit too old to be called \"the next big thing.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Basically the tech industry seems to be in an awkward period now. \"There is not any one-hit wonder, and there will not be one for years to come,\" said Gary Shapiro, president and chief executive of the Consumer Technology Association (CTA). In his eyes, however, that doesn't necessarily mean that innovation has stopped. It has just grown up a little. \"Many industries are going out of infancy and becoming adolescents,\" Shapiro said."}, {"label": "D", "text": "For instance, new technologies that are building upon existing technology have not found their footing well enough to appeal to a mass audience, because, in many cases, they need to work effectively with other devices to realize their full appeal. Take the evolution of the smart home, for example. Companies are pushing it hard but make it almost overwhelming even to dip a toe in the water for the average consumer, because there are so many compatibility issues to think about. No average person wants to figure out whether their favorite calendar software works with their fridge or whether their washing machine and tablet get along. Having to install a different app for each smart appliance in your home is annoying; it would be nicer if you could manage everything together. And while you may forgive your smartphone an occasional fault, you probably have less patience for error messages from your door lock."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Companies are promoting their own standards, and the market has not had time to choose a winner yet as this is still very new. Companies that have long focused on hardware now have to think of ecosystems instead to give consumers practical solutions to their everyday problems. \"The dialogue is changing from what is technologically possible to what is technologically meaningful,\" said economist Shawn DuBravac. DuBravac works for CTA—which puts on the show each year—and said that this shift to a search for solutions has been noticeable as he researched his predictions for 2016."}, {"label": "F", "text": "\"So much of what CES has been about is the cool. It is about the flashiness and the gadgets,\" said John Curran, managing director of research at Accenture. \"But over the last couple of years, and in this one in particular, we are starting to see companies shift from what is the largest screen size, the smallest form factor or the shiniest object and more into what all of these devices do that is practical in a consumer's life.\" Even the technology press conferences, which have been high-profile in the past and reached a level of drama and theatrics fitting for a Las Vegas stage, have a different bent to them. Rather than just dazzling with a high cool factor, there is a focus on the practical. Fitbit, for example, released its first smartwatch Monday, selling with a clear purpose—to improve your fitness—and promoting it as a \"tool, not a toy.\" Not only that, it supports a number of platforms: Apple's IOS, Google's Android and Microsoft's Windows phone."}, {"label": "G", "text": "That seems to be what consumers are demanding, after all. Consumers are becoming increasingly bored with what companies have to offer: A survey of 28,000 consumers in 28 countries released by Accenture found consumers are not as excited about technology as they once were. For example, when asked whether they would buy a new smartphone this year, only 48 percent said yes—a six-point drop from 2015."}, {"label": "H", "text": "And when it comes to the hyper-connected super-smart world that technology firms are painting for us, it seems that consumers are growing more uneasy about handing over the massive amounts of consumer data needed to provide the personalized, customized solutions that companies need to improve their services. That could be another explanation for why companies seem to be strengthening their talk of the practicality of their devices."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Companies have already won part of the battle, having driven tech into every part of our lives, tracking our steps and our very heartbeats. Yet the persistent question of \"Why do I need that?\"—or, perhaps more tellingly, \"Why do you need to know that?\"—dogs the steps of many new ventures. Only 13 percent of respondents said that they were interested in buying a smartwatch in 2016, for example—an increase of just one percent from the previous year despite a year of high-profile launches. That is bad news for any firm that may hope that smartwatches can make up ground for maturing smartphone and tablet markets. And the survey found flat demand for fitness monitors, smart thermostats(恒温器) and connected home cameras, as well."}, {"label": "J", "text": "According to the survey, that lack of enthusiasm could stem from concerns about privacy and security. Even among people who have bought connected devices of some kind, 37 percent said that they are going to be more cautious about using these devices and services in the future. A full 18 percent have even returned devices until they feel they can get safer guarantees against having their sensitive information hacked."}, {"label": "K", "text": "That, too, explains the heavy Washington presence at this year's show, as these new technologies intrude upon heavily regulated areas. In addition to many senior officials from the Federal Trade and Federal Communications commissions, this year's list of policy makers also includes appearances from Transportation Secretary Anthony Foxx, to talk about smart cities, and Federal Aviation Administration Administrator Michael Huerta, to talk about drones."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Curran, the Accenture analyst, said that increased government interest in the show makes sense as technology becomes a larger part of our lives. \"There is an incompatibility in the rate at which these are advancing relative to the way we're digesting it,\" he said. \"Technology is becoming bigger and more aspirational, and penetrating almost every aspect of our lives. We have to understand and think about the implications, and balance these great innovations with the potential downsides they naturally carry with them.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The Paris climate agreement finalised in December last year heralded a new era for climate action. For the first time, the world's nations agreed to keep global warming well below 2℃.\nThis is vital for climate-vulnerable nations. Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's greenhouse gas emissions. In a study published in Nature Scientific Reports, we reveal just how deep this injustice runs.\nDeveloped nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are essentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high greenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water. In other words, a few countries are benefiting enormously from the consumption of fossil fuels, while at the same time contributing disproportionately to the global burden of climate change.\nOn the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. Many of the world's most climate vulnerable countries, the majority of which are African or small island states, produce a very small quantity of emissions. This is much like a non-smoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough to smoke in good health.\nThe Paris agreement has been widely hailed as a positive step forward in addressing climate change for all, although the details on addressing \"climate justice\" can be best described as sketchy.\nThe goal of keeping global temperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable but the emissions-reduction pledges submitted by countries leading up to the Paris talks are very unlikely to deliver on this.\nMore than $100 billion in funding has been put on the table for supporting developing nations to reduce emissions. However, the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between developed and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions, effectively ignoring historical emissions. There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is responsible for their provision. Securing these funds, and establishing who is responsible for raising them will also be vital for the future of climate-vulnerable countries.\nThe most climate-vulnerable countries in the world have contributed very little to creating the global disease from which they now suffer the most. There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if we are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries adapt to climate change.\nAnd it is clearly up to the current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide whether they want to be remembered as climate change tyrants or pioneers.", "47": "The Paris climate agreement finalised in December last year heralded a new era for climate action. For the first time, the world's nations agreed to keep global warming well below 2℃.\nThis is vital for climate-vulnerable nations. Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's greenhouse gas emissions. In a study published in Nature Scientific Reports, we reveal just how deep this injustice runs.\nDeveloped nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are essentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high greenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water. In other words, a few countries are benefiting enormously from the consumption of fossil fuels, while at the same time contributing disproportionately to the global burden of climate change.\nOn the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. Many of the world's most climate vulnerable countries, the majority of which are African or small island states, produce a very small quantity of emissions. This is much like a non-smoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough to smoke in good health.\nThe Paris agreement has been widely hailed as a positive step forward in addressing climate change for all, although the details on addressing \"climate justice\" can be best described as sketchy.\nThe goal of keeping global temperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable but the emissions-reduction pledges submitted by countries leading up to the Paris talks are very unlikely to deliver on this.\nMore than $100 billion in funding has been put on the table for supporting developing nations to reduce emissions. However, the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between developed and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions, effectively ignoring historical emissions. There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is responsible for their provision. Securing these funds, and establishing who is responsible for raising them will also be vital for the future of climate-vulnerable countries.\nThe most climate-vulnerable countries in the world have contributed very little to creating the global disease from which they now suffer the most. There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if we are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries adapt to climate change.\nAnd it is clearly up to the current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide whether they want to be remembered as climate change tyrants or pioneers.", "48": "The Paris climate agreement finalised in December last year heralded a new era for climate action. For the first time, the world's nations agreed to keep global warming well below 2℃.\nThis is vital for climate-vulnerable nations. Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's greenhouse gas emissions. In a study published in Nature Scientific Reports, we reveal just how deep this injustice runs.\nDeveloped nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are essentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high greenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water. In other words, a few countries are benefiting enormously from the consumption of fossil fuels, while at the same time contributing disproportionately to the global burden of climate change.\nOn the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. Many of the world's most climate vulnerable countries, the majority of which are African or small island states, produce a very small quantity of emissions. This is much like a non-smoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough to smoke in good health.\nThe Paris agreement has been widely hailed as a positive step forward in addressing climate change for all, although the details on addressing \"climate justice\" can be best described as sketchy.\nThe goal of keeping global temperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable but the emissions-reduction pledges submitted by countries leading up to the Paris talks are very unlikely to deliver on this.\nMore than $100 billion in funding has been put on the table for supporting developing nations to reduce emissions. However, the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between developed and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions, effectively ignoring historical emissions. There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is responsible for their provision. Securing these funds, and establishing who is responsible for raising them will also be vital for the future of climate-vulnerable countries.\nThe most climate-vulnerable countries in the world have contributed very little to creating the global disease from which they now suffer the most. There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if we are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries adapt to climate change.\nAnd it is clearly up to the current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide whether they want to be remembered as climate change tyrants or pioneers.", "49": "The Paris climate agreement finalised in December last year heralded a new era for climate action. For the first time, the world's nations agreed to keep global warming well below 2℃.\nThis is vital for climate-vulnerable nations. Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's greenhouse gas emissions. In a study published in Nature Scientific Reports, we reveal just how deep this injustice runs.\nDeveloped nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are essentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high greenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water. In other words, a few countries are benefiting enormously from the consumption of fossil fuels, while at the same time contributing disproportionately to the global burden of climate change.\nOn the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. Many of the world's most climate vulnerable countries, the majority of which are African or small island states, produce a very small quantity of emissions. This is much like a non-smoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough to smoke in good health.\nThe Paris agreement has been widely hailed as a positive step forward in addressing climate change for all, although the details on addressing \"climate justice\" can be best described as sketchy.\nThe goal of keeping global temperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable but the emissions-reduction pledges submitted by countries leading up to the Paris talks are very unlikely to deliver on this.\nMore than $100 billion in funding has been put on the table for supporting developing nations to reduce emissions. However, the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between developed and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions, effectively ignoring historical emissions. There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is responsible for their provision. Securing these funds, and establishing who is responsible for raising them will also be vital for the future of climate-vulnerable countries.\nThe most climate-vulnerable countries in the world have contributed very little to creating the global disease from which they now suffer the most. There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if we are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries adapt to climate change.\nAnd it is clearly up to the current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide whether they want to be remembered as climate change tyrants or pioneers.", "50": "The Paris climate agreement finalised in December last year heralded a new era for climate action. For the first time, the world's nations agreed to keep global warming well below 2℃.\nThis is vital for climate-vulnerable nations. Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's greenhouse gas emissions. In a study published in Nature Scientific Reports, we reveal just how deep this injustice runs.\nDeveloped nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are essentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high greenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water. In other words, a few countries are benefiting enormously from the consumption of fossil fuels, while at the same time contributing disproportionately to the global burden of climate change.\nOn the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. Many of the world's most climate vulnerable countries, the majority of which are African or small island states, produce a very small quantity of emissions. This is much like a non-smoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough to smoke in good health.\nThe Paris agreement has been widely hailed as a positive step forward in addressing climate change for all, although the details on addressing \"climate justice\" can be best described as sketchy.\nThe goal of keeping global temperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable but the emissions-reduction pledges submitted by countries leading up to the Paris talks are very unlikely to deliver on this.\nMore than $100 billion in funding has been put on the table for supporting developing nations to reduce emissions. However, the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between developed and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions, effectively ignoring historical emissions. There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is responsible for their provision. Securing these funds, and establishing who is responsible for raising them will also be vital for the future of climate-vulnerable countries.\nThe most climate-vulnerable countries in the world have contributed very little to creating the global disease from which they now suffer the most. There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if we are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries adapt to climate change.\nAnd it is clearly up to the current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide whether they want to be remembered as climate change tyrants or pioneers.", "51": "Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking cigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems are brewing.\nBut a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\nOf course, that may sound like a description of every teenager on the planet. But the study warns that it is teenagers who engage in all three of these practices in the extreme who are truly in jeopardy. Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been dubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n\"In some ways they're at greater risk of falling through the cracks,\" says researcher Vladimir Carli. \"While most parents, teachers and clinicians would react to an adolescent using drugs or getting drunk, they may easily overlook teenagers who are engaging in inconspicuous behaviors.\"\nThe study's authors surveyed 12,395 students and analyzed nine risk behaviors, including excessive alcohol use, illegal drug use, heavy smoking, high media use and truancy(逃学). Their aim was to determine the relationship between these risk behaviors and mental health issues in teenagers.\nAbout 58% of the students demonstrated none or few of the risk behaviors. Some 13% scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors. And 29%, the \"invisible risk\" group, scored high on three in particular: They spent five hours a day or more on electronic devices. They slept six hours a night or less. And they neglected \"other healthy activities.\"\nThe group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show symptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared with just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set, with more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\nThe findings caught Carli off guard. \"We were very surprised,\" he says. \"The high-risk group and low-risk group are obvious. But this third group was not only unexpected, it was so distinct and so large—nearly one third of our sample—that it became a key finding of the study.\"\nCarli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers. And early identification, support and treatment for mental health issues, he says, are the best ways to keep them from turning into full-blown disorders.", "52": "Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking cigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems are brewing.\nBut a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\nOf course, that may sound like a description of every teenager on the planet. But the study warns that it is teenagers who engage in all three of these practices in the extreme who are truly in jeopardy. Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been dubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n\"In some ways they're at greater risk of falling through the cracks,\" says researcher Vladimir Carli. \"While most parents, teachers and clinicians would react to an adolescent using drugs or getting drunk, they may easily overlook teenagers who are engaging in inconspicuous behaviors.\"\nThe study's authors surveyed 12,395 students and analyzed nine risk behaviors, including excessive alcohol use, illegal drug use, heavy smoking, high media use and truancy(逃学). Their aim was to determine the relationship between these risk behaviors and mental health issues in teenagers.\nAbout 58% of the students demonstrated none or few of the risk behaviors. Some 13% scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors. And 29%, the \"invisible risk\" group, scored high on three in particular: They spent five hours a day or more on electronic devices. They slept six hours a night or less. And they neglected \"other healthy activities.\"\nThe group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show symptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared with just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set, with more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\nThe findings caught Carli off guard. \"We were very surprised,\" he says. \"The high-risk group and low-risk group are obvious. But this third group was not only unexpected, it was so distinct and so large—nearly one third of our sample—that it became a key finding of the study.\"\nCarli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers. And early identification, support and treatment for mental health issues, he says, are the best ways to keep them from turning into full-blown disorders.", "53": "Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking cigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems are brewing.\nBut a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\nOf course, that may sound like a description of every teenager on the planet. But the study warns that it is teenagers who engage in all three of these practices in the extreme who are truly in jeopardy. Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been dubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n\"In some ways they're at greater risk of falling through the cracks,\" says researcher Vladimir Carli. \"While most parents, teachers and clinicians would react to an adolescent using drugs or getting drunk, they may easily overlook teenagers who are engaging in inconspicuous behaviors.\"\nThe study's authors surveyed 12,395 students and analyzed nine risk behaviors, including excessive alcohol use, illegal drug use, heavy smoking, high media use and truancy(逃学). Their aim was to determine the relationship between these risk behaviors and mental health issues in teenagers.\nAbout 58% of the students demonstrated none or few of the risk behaviors. Some 13% scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors. And 29%, the \"invisible risk\" group, scored high on three in particular: They spent five hours a day or more on electronic devices. They slept six hours a night or less. And they neglected \"other healthy activities.\"\nThe group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show symptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared with just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set, with more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\nThe findings caught Carli off guard. \"We were very surprised,\" he says. \"The high-risk group and low-risk group are obvious. But this third group was not only unexpected, it was so distinct and so large—nearly one third of our sample—that it became a key finding of the study.\"\nCarli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers. And early identification, support and treatment for mental health issues, he says, are the best ways to keep them from turning into full-blown disorders.", "54": "Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking cigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems are brewing.\nBut a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\nOf course, that may sound like a description of every teenager on the planet. But the study warns that it is teenagers who engage in all three of these practices in the extreme who are truly in jeopardy. Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been dubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n\"In some ways they're at greater risk of falling through the cracks,\" says researcher Vladimir Carli. \"While most parents, teachers and clinicians would react to an adolescent using drugs or getting drunk, they may easily overlook teenagers who are engaging in inconspicuous behaviors.\"\nThe study's authors surveyed 12,395 students and analyzed nine risk behaviors, including excessive alcohol use, illegal drug use, heavy smoking, high media use and truancy(逃学). Their aim was to determine the relationship between these risk behaviors and mental health issues in teenagers.\nAbout 58% of the students demonstrated none or few of the risk behaviors. Some 13% scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors. And 29%, the \"invisible risk\" group, scored high on three in particular: They spent five hours a day or more on electronic devices. They slept six hours a night or less. And they neglected \"other healthy activities.\"\nThe group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show symptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared with just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set, with more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\nThe findings caught Carli off guard. \"We were very surprised,\" he says. \"The high-risk group and low-risk group are obvious. But this third group was not only unexpected, it was so distinct and so large—nearly one third of our sample—that it became a key finding of the study.\"\nCarli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers. And early identification, support and treatment for mental health issues, he says, are the best ways to keep them from turning into full-blown disorders.", "55": "Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking cigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems are brewing.\nBut a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\nOf course, that may sound like a description of every teenager on the planet. But the study warns that it is teenagers who engage in all three of these practices in the extreme who are truly in jeopardy. Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been dubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n\"In some ways they're at greater risk of falling through the cracks,\" says researcher Vladimir Carli. \"While most parents, teachers and clinicians would react to an adolescent using drugs or getting drunk, they may easily overlook teenagers who are engaging in inconspicuous behaviors.\"\nThe study's authors surveyed 12,395 students and analyzed nine risk behaviors, including excessive alcohol use, illegal drug use, heavy smoking, high media use and truancy(逃学). Their aim was to determine the relationship between these risk behaviors and mental health issues in teenagers.\nAbout 58% of the students demonstrated none or few of the risk behaviors. Some 13% scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors. And 29%, the \"invisible risk\" group, scored high on three in particular: They spent five hours a day or more on electronic devices. They slept six hours a night or less. And they neglected \"other healthy activities.\"\nThe group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show symptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared with just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set, with more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\nThe findings caught Carli off guard. \"We were very surprised,\" he says. \"The high-risk group and low-risk group are obvious. But this third group was not only unexpected, it was so distinct and so large—nearly one third of our sample—that it became a key finding of the study.\"\nCarli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers. And early identification, support and treatment for mental health issues, he says, are the best ways to keep them from turning into full-blown disorders."}, "questions": [{"id": "2016-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does Professor Henderson say about his main area of research?", "options": ["A) It tries to predict the possible trends of global climate change.", "B) It studies the impacts of global climate change on people's lives.", "C) It links the science of climate change to economic and policy issues.", "D) It focuses on the efforts countries can make to deal with global warming."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It links the science of climate change to economic and policy issues.\n【定位】The Center for Climate Research where I work links the science of climate change to issues\naround economics and policy.\n我工作的气候研究中心把气候变化的科学与围绕经济和政策的问题联系起来。\n【信号】被问者对开场提问的直接应答。女士在第 1 段请教授概述其工作与研究，教授开口第一句\nlinks the science of climate change to issues around economics and policy 就是对该\n提问的正面应答，问\"研究是什么\"，答案必落在他自述研究内容的这句。\n【替换】links the science of climate change to issues around economics and policy ↔\nlinks the science of climate change to economic and policy issues\n近乎原句，仅词序与词形微调\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 predict the possible trends 在录音里没有对应——教授说的是\"连接\"科学与经济政\n策，并非\"预测趋势\"，属无中生有的拔高。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段确有 the likely impacts of climate change，B 借走 impacts 拼成 impacts of\nglobal climate change on people's lives；但原文的 impacts 是研究的\"附带内容之一\"，\n且从未限定到 on people's lives，B 听到 impacts 就选会答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：the likely impacts of climate change and all of the associated risks\nD) 张冠李戴\nefforts countries can make 借用了后文 action on a global scale 的\"各国行动\"概念，但\n那是教授谈\"怎么办\"时说的，并非他描述\"研究领域\"的内容，张冠李戴到本题。\n借⽤原⽂：we may need action on a global scale", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does Professor Henderson say about climate change?", "options": ["A) It will take a long time before a consensus is reached on its impact.", "B) It would be more costly to deal with its consequences than to avoid it.", "C) It is the most pressing issue confronting all countries.", "D) It is bound to cause endless disputes among nations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It would be more costly to deal with its consequences than to avoid it.\n【定位】What the economics tells us is that it's probably cheaper to avoid climate change—to avoid\nthe risk—than it is to deal with the likely consequences.\n经济学告诉我们的是，避免气候变化（即避免风险）很可能比应对其可能的后果更划算。\n【信号】比较结构 cheaper...than 给出结论。教授用 cheaper to avoid climate change... than it\nis to deal with the likely consequences 这一 cheaper...than 比较句下了核心判断，比较\n结构正是答案信号——它把\"避免\"和\"应对\"两种做法的代价摆出来对比。\n【替换】cheaper to avoid climate change... than it is to deal with the likely consequences ↔\nmore costly to deal with its consequences than to avoid it\n比较方向对调＋反义词替换，等值\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音末尾确有 we don't have that consensus，A 借 consensus 编成 a long time before\na consensus is reached on its impact；但原文那处指的是\"各国对全球协议尚无共识\"，不\n是\"对气候影响要很久才有共识\"，听到 consensus 就选即落陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：at the moment we don't have that consensus\nC) 过度推断\nthe most pressing issue confronting all countries 用了最高级\"最紧迫\"，但教授全程只\n说气候变化是\"风险\"、避免更划算，从未把它定为\"所有国家最紧迫的问题\"，属拔高。\nD) 过度推断\n录音第 4 段说部分科学 less certain or at least more disputed，D 借 disputed 推成\nendless disputes among nations；但原文是\"科学层面存在争议\"，并非\"国家间必然无尽\n争吵\"，由词推断过头。\n借⽤原⽂：other aspects of the science are less certain or at least more disputed", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does Professor Henderson say is a top priority in combating climate change?", "options": ["A) The transition to low-carbon energy systems.", "B) The cooperation among world major powers.", "C) The signing of a global agreement.", "D) The raising of people's awareness."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The transition to low-carbon energy systems.\n【定位】And this will involve a huge transition to low-carbon energy systems. And the transition is a\ntremendous priority.\n这将涉及向低碳能源系统的巨大转型。而这一转型是一项极其重要的首要任务。\n【信号】优先级标记 a tremendous priority。教授先抛出 transition to low-carbon energy\nsystems，紧接着用 the transition is a tremendous priority 明确给它贴上\"首要\"标签\n——priority 这个词就是答案信号，它指认的措施才是被问的 top priority。\n【替换】a huge transition to low-carbon energy systems ↔ The transition to low-carbon energy systems\n近乎原词照搬，仅去掉修饰\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音提到 major countries like China and the USA，B 借此编成 cooperation among\nworld major powers；但原文说的是大国\"签署全球协议\"，并非笼统的\"大国合作\"，更未称\n之为首要任务。\n借⽤原⽂：major countries like China and the USA might sign up to a global agreement\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文确有 sign up to a global agreement，C 直接搬来 The signing of a global\nagreement；但被冠以 priority 的是\"转型\"，签协议只是从政治角度需要理解的条件，不是\n教授点名的首要任务，听到原词就选会错。\n借⽤原⽂：major countries like China and the USA might sign up to a global agreement\nD) 未提及\nThe raising of people's awareness 在整段录音里没有任何对应表述，属凭\"环保常识\"自行\n补出的措施。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does Professor Henderson advise us to do to better deal with climate change?", "options": ["A) Carry out more research on it.", "B) Cut down energy consumption.", "C) Plan well in advance.", "D) Adopt new technology."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Plan well in advance.\n【定位】And we also need to plan ahead so that we are in a position to deal with the likely levels of\nclimate change which are already inevitable.\n我们还需要提前规划，以便能够应对那些已经不可避免的气候变化可能达到的程度。\n【信号】建议句 we also need to + 动作。教授在收尾段用 we also need to plan ahead 这一 we\nneed to 建议句给出最终的\"更好应对\"之道——need to 后面的动作 plan ahead 就是答案\n信号所在。\n【替换】we also need to plan ahead ↔ Plan well in advance\nplan ahead 同义改写为 plan well in advance\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第 6 段有 develop the science、第 2 段有 research 字样，A 的 Carry out more\nresearch on it 借此而来；但\"发展科学/研究\"是教授谈\"怎么避免\"时说的，本题问\"更好应\n对\"，他给的建议是提前规划，A 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：we need to develop the science specifically to understand the likely impacts\nB) 偷换概念\nCut down energy consumption 看似呼应 low-carbon energy systems，但原文谈的是\n能源系统\"转型\"，从未说要\"削减能耗\"，且这是第 3 题话题，并非本题的应对建议。\n借⽤原⽂：a huge transition to low-carbon energy systems\nD) 未提及\nAdopt new technology 在录音里没有对应——教授谈的是发展科学、能源转型、提前规\n划，从未说\"采用新技术\"，属常识补全。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) When luck plays a role.", "B) What determines success.", "C) Whether practice makes perfect.", "D) How important natural talent is."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） What determines success.\n【定位】We often end up talking about things like success and what leads to success.\n我们常常聊着聊着就谈到成功、以及是什么带来成功这类话题。\n【信号】开头点题句。对话第一段女士就抛出 talking about things like success and what leads\nto success，这是开篇点题句；主旨题答案多在开头这种\"奠定话题\"的句子里，后面全程都\n在围着它展开。\n【替换】what leads to success ↔ What determines success\nleads to = determines\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 Luck is important to success，让人想选 When luck plays a role.；但运气\n只是\"成功要素\"里被讨论的一个侧面，不是整段主题，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：Luck is important to success\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后段反复说 It's all about practice, practice, practice，诱人选 Whether practice\nmakes perfect.；但练习只是讨论\"成功要素\"时谈到的一环，且对话并非在辩论\"练习是否使\n人完美\"，范围对不上主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：It's all about practice, practice, practice\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音谈到 natural talent is something you must have in order to be successful，让人\n想选 How important natural talent is.；但天赋同样只是被讨论的一个要素，不能代表整\n段\"成功由什么决定\"的全貌。\n借⽤原⽂：natural talent is something you must have in order to be successful", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What is the woman's view of luck?", "options": ["A) It knocks at your door only once in a while.", "B) It is something that no one can possibly create.", "C) It comes naturally out of one's self-confidence.", "D) It means being good at seizing opportunities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It means being good at seizing opportunities.\n【定位】I think a lot of what people consider luck is attributed to how you respond to the opportunities\nthat come your way.\n我认为，人们所谓的运气，很大程度上取决于你如何回应向你迎面而来的机会。\n【信号】I think 表态句。女士用 I think a lot of what people consider luck is attributed to how\nyou respond to the opportunities 亮明自己观点，I think 是观点题的标准信号；态度题\n就抓\"那个人\"亲口 I think / I believe 的那句。\n【替换】how you respond to the opportunities that come your way ↔ being good at seizing opportunities\n积极回应机会=会抓机会\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt knocks at your door only once in a while. 听着像\"运气可遇不可求\"的常识，但女士全\n程没说运气\"偶尔才来\"，她说的是\"运气取决于你怎么回应机会\"，这是拿常识硬套、原文没\n说。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is something that no one can possibly create. 说\"运气没人能创造\"；可录音里说\ncreate 的是男士 people can make their own luck，且他认为运气\"可以\"创造，选项却\n说\"没人能\"——既张冠李戴又反了意。\n借⽤原⽂：people can make their own luck\nC) 未提及\nIt comes naturally out of one's self-confidence. 把运气说成\"源于自信\"，但 self-\nconfidence 在录音里根本没出现，女士谈的是\"回应机会\"不是\"自信\"，纯属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the chief point the TED Talks video makes?", "options": ["A) Luck rarely contributes to a person's success.", "B) One must have natural talent to be successful.", "C) One should always be ready to seize opportunities.", "D) Practice is essential to becoming good at something."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Practice is essential to becoming good at something.\n【定位】The point about getting good at something is not about having some natural talent. It's all\nabout practice, practice, practice.\n关于把一件事做好，重点不在于拥有什么天赋，而完全在于练习、练习、再练习。\n【信号】not…but 转折加重复。录音用 not about having some natural talent. It's all about\npractice, practice, practice 这个\"否定天赋、强调练习\"的转折结构，又把 practice 连说三\n遍加重语气；转折后 + 重复强调，正是答案落点。\n【替换】It's all about practice, practice, practice ↔ Practice is essential to becoming good at something\n全靠练习=练习必不可少\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nLuck rarely contributes to a person's success. 里 rarely（几乎不）极端化了；录音里女\n士还说 Luck is important to success，并未否定运气，更没说视频持\"运气几乎无用\"的论\n点，程度与出处都不符。\n借⽤原⽂：Luck is important to success\nB) 原词陷阱\nOne must have natural talent to be successful. 借用了录音原词 natural talent，但视频\n的论点恰恰是 not about having some natural talent——否定\"必须有天赋\"；选项取了被\n否定的那半，正好说反。\n借⽤原⽂：not about having some natural talent\nC) 张冠李戴\nOne should always be ready to seize opportunities. 对应的是女士第5段 how you\nrespond to the opportunities 的观点，而本题问的是\"视频的论点\"；抓机会是女士谈运气\n时说的，不是视频提出的，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：how you respond to the opportunities that come your way", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman think is the funny thing in the TED Talks video?", "options": ["A) Putting time and effort into fun things is profitable.", "B) People who love what they do care little about money.", "C) Being passionate about work can make one wealthy.", "D) People in need of money work hard automatically."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Being passionate about work can make one wealthy.\n【定位】If you love what you do and are really passionate about it and work really hard, the money\nkind of comes automatically.\n如果你热爱自己所做的事、对它充满热情并且非常努力地工作，钱财就会自然而然地随之\n而来。\n【信号】the funny thing 强调句。女士先说 the funny thing is that 引出要点，随后 if you love\nwhat you do…work really hard, the money kind of comes automatically 给出那件趣\n事；funny thing is that 是明确的强调信号，紧跟其后的就是答案句。\n【替换】if you love what you do…the money kind of comes automatically ↔\nBeing passionate about work can make one wealthy\n热爱→钱自来=热情使人富\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nPutting time and effort into fun things is profitable. 把录音的\"热爱所做的工作\"换成了\nfun things（好玩的事），还把\"钱自然来\"换成 profitable；录音说的是 love what you do\n（热爱你做的事）不是\"做好玩的事\"，概念被悄悄换掉。\n借⽤原⽂：put the time and effort into it\nB) 原词陷阱\nPeople who love what they do care little about money. 借用录音原词 love what they\ndo，却生造出\"不在乎钱\"；录音说的是\"钱会自然来\"，根本没说\"不在乎钱\"，是凭这个原词\n钩子无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：there're people who really love what they do\nD) 与原文相反\nPeople in need of money work hard automatically. 把因果说成\"缺钱→努力\"，而录音\n是\"热爱努力→钱自来\"，方向正好相反；它还盗用了原词 automatically 来增加迷惑。\n借⽤原⽂：the money kind of comes automatically", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does Devils Tower look like?", "options": ["A) The stump of a giant tree.", "B) A huge piece of rock.", "C) The peak of a mountain.", "D) A tall chimney."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The stump of a giant tree.\n【定位】Devils Tower, the first national monument in America, could almost be mistaken for the\nstump of an enormous tree.\n魔鬼塔，美国第一座国家纪念碑，几乎会被错看成一棵巨树的树桩。\n【信号】“被错看成”即外形比喻信号。could almost be mistaken for（几乎会被错看成）是一个明\n确的“像什么”信号词，听到它后面紧跟的那个比喻对象，就是本题答案落点。\n【替换】the stump of an enormous tree ↔ The stump of a giant tree.\nenormous = giant 近义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实多处出现 rock（如 the massive column of rock），B 的 A huge piece of rock.\n盗用了这个原词诱你“听到 rock 就选”；但题目问“看上去像什么”，原文给的比喻是“树桩”而\n非“一块巨石”，rock 只是它的材质而非它“像”的东西。\n借⽤原⽂：the massive column of rock\nC) 偷换概念\n原文提到它矗立在平原之上、有 flat summit（平顶），让人联想到山，C 的 The peak of\na mountain. 借“山峰/顶”的语境诱选；但原文从未说它“像一座山峰”，比喻对象明确是“树\n桩”。\n借⽤原⽂：the flat summit\nD) 未提及\nA tall chimney.（高烟囱）这一比喻在原文中根本没有出现，纯属编造，虽然柱状高耸的形\n状会让人觉得“也挺像”，但听力以原文说了什么为准。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What caused the volcano's outer layers to wear away?", "options": ["A) Human activity.", "B) Wind and water.", "C) Chemical processes.", "D) Fire and fury."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Wind and water.\n【定位】Born in fire and fury, Devils Tower was then shaped by the slow, gentle work of wind\nand water. The outer layers of the volcano were worn away, until the hard core stood\ncompletely exposed.\n魔鬼塔生于烈火与狂暴之中，随后被风与水缓慢而轻柔的作用所塑造。火山的外层逐渐被磨\n蚀殆尽，直到坚硬的内核完全裸露出来。\n【信号】shaped by + worn away 的施动者。shaped by the slow, gentle work of wind and\nwater 里的 shaped by 点出真正的施动力量；紧接着 The outer layers of the volcano\nwere worn away 用被动语态说外层被磨蚀——“被…塑造/被磨蚀”的施动者就是答案信号。\n【替换】wind and water ↔ Wind and water.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHuman activity.（人类活动）在原文里完全没有提到，魔鬼塔的塑造全是自然力量；这一项\n靠常识里“景观破坏常与人类有关”的联想骗选。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文讲 the rock cooled and hardened（岩石冷却硬化）涉及物理变化，C 的 Chemical\nprocesses.（化学过程）借“岩石变化”语境偷换成“化学过程”；但磨蚀外层的是风与水的物\n理作用，非化学过程。\n借⽤原⽂：the rock cooled and hardened\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 Born in fire and fury 确有 fire and fury 一词，D 的 Fire and fury. 原样盗用，诱\n你“听到就选”；但“烈火与狂暴”说的是它“诞生（born in）”时的力量，问的却是“外层被磨\n蚀”的原因，张冠李戴、答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Born in fire and fury", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does an Indian legend say about Devils Tower?", "options": ["A) It is a historical monument.", "B) It was built in ancient times.", "C) It is Indians' sacred place for worship.", "D) It was created by supernatural powers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was created by supernatural powers.\n【定位】Small wonder that an Indian legend described Devils Tower as being formed by\nsupernatural powers.\n难怪一则印第安传说把魔鬼塔描述为由超自然力量所形成。\n【信号】legend described…as 引出传说界定。an Indian legend described Devils Tower as\nbeing formed by supernatural powers 里的 legend described…as（传说把它描述\n为…）是明确的“传说怎么说”信号，as 后面的内容就是传说的界定，即答案落点。\n【替换】being formed by supernatural powers ↔ It was created by supernatural powers.\nformed = created 近义、被动同构\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头说它是 the first national monument in America，A 的 It is a historical\nmonument. 盗用 monument 一词诱选；但 monument 是“国家纪念地”的官方身份，题\n目问的是“传说怎么说”，传说讲的是超自然力量，不是“历史纪念碑”。\n借⽤原⽂：the first national monument in America\nB) 与原文相反\nIt was built in ancient times.（古时人工建造）与传说“由超自然力量形成”相反——传说强\n调的恰是“非人力所及”，built（人建）正好反了向；它借“ancient/古老”的氛围诱选。\n借⽤原⽂：being formed by supernatural powers\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音多次出现 Indian（如 an Indian legend），C 的 It is Indians' sacred place for\nworship. 盗用 Indian 诱选；但原文只说“印第安传说”提到它，从未说它是印第安人“供奉/\n朝拜的圣地”，sacred place for worship 属编造。\n借⽤原⽂：an Indian legend", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "How did the Rock God help the seven girls in the Indian legend?", "options": ["A) By sheltering them in a cave.", "B) By killing the attacking bears.", "C) By lifting them well above the ground.", "D) By taking them to the top of a mountain."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By lifting them well above the ground.\n【定位】The god caused the rock to grow and to lift the girls far above the ground, while its sides were\nscored by the claws of the angry bears.\n岩神让岩石长高，把女孩们高高托到地面之上，而岩石的侧面则被愤怒的熊用爪子抓出道道\n划痕。\n【信号】caused the rock to…lift 的施救动作。The god caused the rock to grow and to lift\nthe girls far above the ground 直接说出岩神所做的事——让岩石长高、把女孩托离地\n面，这句“岩神 + 动作”就是 How 题的答案落点。\n【替换】lift the girls far above the ground ↔ By lifting them well above the ground.\nlift…far/well above the ground 近义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\nBy sheltering them in a cave.（把她们藏进山洞）在原文里没有出现，岩神是“让岩石长高\n托起她们”而非“藏入山洞”；这项靠“躲避熊”的常识联想骗选。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里确有 the claws of the angry bears（愤怒的熊的爪子），B 的 By killing the\nattacking bears. 盗用 bears 诱选；但原文是“熊用爪子抓岩石侧面”，岩神并没有“杀熊”，\n把不存在的动作安到岩神头上，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：the claws of the angry bears\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是让岩石长高、把女孩 lift the girls far above the ground（托到地面之上），D 的\nBy taking them to the top of a mountain. 把它偷换成“带到山顶”；岩神是“就地把岩石升\n高”而非“带她们去某座山顶”，似是而非。\n借⽤原⽂：lift the girls far above the ground", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about drivers who stop at gas stations?", "options": ["A) They will buy something from the convenience stores.", "B) They will take advantage of the time to rest a while.", "C) They will have their vehicles washed or serviced.", "D) They will pick up some souvenirs or gift items."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They will buy something from the convenience stores.\n【定位】Few of us can fill up the tank without buying a few snacks, cigarettes, soft drinks or other\nitems we can live without.\n我们当中很少有人能在加油时不顺手买点零食、香烟、软饮料或其他可有可无的东西。\n【信号】双重否定 Few…without 即“几乎都会买”。信号是这句的双重否定结构 Few of us can fill\nup the tank without buying：few（很少有人能）+ without buying（不买）两个否定叠\n加，实际等于“几乎人人都会买点东西”，这正是对司机行为的正面回答。\n【替换】buying a few snacks, cigarettes, soft drinks or other items ↔\nbuy something from the convenience stores\n罗列具体商品概括为 something\n【排除】B)\n选项 take advantage of the time to rest a while 说司机利用时间休息，但录音通篇只讲\n加油时“买东西”，从未提及休息，属凭常识脑补、原文未提及。\nC)\n选项 have their vehicles washed or serviced（洗车、做保养）借“加油站”让人自然联想到\n的配套服务来诱选，但录音只字未提洗车或保养，是借场景联想编出来的未提及项。\nD)\n选项 pick up some souvenirs or gift items（买纪念品或礼品）与录音所列的 snacks、\ncigarettes、soft drinks 完全不同类，录音也没提纪念品，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the speaker say about extravagances?", "options": ["A) They can bring only temporary pleasures.", "B) They are meant for the extremely wealthy.", "C) They should be done away with altogether.", "D) They may eventually drive one to bankruptcy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They can bring only temporary pleasures.\n【定位】Sadly, the pleasure that comes from extravagances often disappears long before the bills do.\n遗憾的是，奢侈消费带来的快乐往往在账单还没还清之前就早早消失了。\n【信号】评价词 Sadly + 主语 pleasure…disappears。信号是句首带态度的副词 Sadly（遗憾地）\n加上正面评价奢侈消费的主句 the pleasure that comes from extravagances often\ndisappears，这正是说话人对 extravagances 的定性句。\n【替换】the pleasure that comes from extravagances often disappears long before the bills do ↔\nbring only temporary pleasures\n快乐很快消失=只带短暂快乐\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项 meant for the extremely wealthy 说奢侈只为极富者，但录音明说 hard-working\nmen and women 这些普通辛劳者也会奢侈消费，与“只给富人”相反。\n借⽤原⽂：hard-working men and women\nC)\n选项 done away with altogether（应彻底取消）把话说过头，录音恰恰承认 They do\ndeserve such indulgences（他们确实配享受这些），只是提醒别忘了更重要的东西，并没\n主张取消，属过度推断、与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：They do deserve such indulgences\nD)\n选项 drive one to bankruptcy 借预读就警惕过的极端词 bankruptcy 诱选，但录音只说快\n乐消失、账单难还（constant stress, tension, even fear about money），从未说会导致\n破产，是把后果夸大、过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：constant stress, tension, even fear about money", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker want to show by the example of the Chicago woman?", "options": ["A) A good way to socialize is to have daily lunch with one's colleagues.", "B) Retirement savings should come first in one's family budgeting.", "C) A vacation will be affordable if one saves 20 dollars a week.", "D) Small daily savings can make a big difference in one's life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题） Small daily savings can make a big difference in one's life.\n【定位】One Chicago woman, for example, discovered that daily lunches with coworkers cost her\n$2000 a year; she decided to take lunch to work instead. \"I now put $20 a week into my\nvacation fund and another 20 into retirement savings.\"\n比如一位芝加哥女士发现每天和同事在外吃午饭一年要花她两千美元，于是改成自己带饭；\n她说“现在我每周往度假基金里存二十美元，再往退休储蓄里存二十美元。”\n【信号】for example 引例，例后承接全段主旨。信号是 for example 标明这是个例子，而它紧跟\n在全段主旨句 The solution—set priorities, add up the annual cost of each item 之后，\n是为印证“算清每项开销、攒小钱”这一主张而举，故例子意图要回到该主旨。\n【替换】daily lunches with coworkers cost her $2000 a year ↔ Small daily savings\n每天省小钱累成一年大数\nput $20 a week into my vacation fund and another 20 into retirement savings ↔\nmake a big difference in one's life\n攒成度假与退休金即大不同\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 have daily lunch with one's colleagues 把录音原词 daily lunches with coworkers\n直接搬来诱选，但她恰恰是“不再天天一起吃、改带饭”，选项却说成是好的社交方式，与原\n文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：daily lunches with coworkers\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Retirement savings should come first 借录音原词 retirement savings 诱选，但例\n子只是把省下的钱同时投入度假与退休两处，并未说退休储蓄要“排第一”，属答非所问、过\n度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：retirement savings\nC)\n选项 saves 20 dollars a week 抓住录音数字 $20 a week 诱选，但录音是说她每周往度假\n基金存 20、往退休存 20，并非“每周省 20 就能负担一次度假”这种因果，属偷换概念、张\n冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：$20 a week into my vacation fund", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about negative emotions?", "options": ["A) They should be done away with.", "B) They are necessary in our lives.", "C) They enrich our experience.", "D) They are harmful to health."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They are necessary in our lives.\n【定位】Feeling both positive and negative emotions is a natural part of being human. We might use\nthe word \"negative\" to describe more difficult emotions, but it doesn't mean those emotions\nare bad or we shouldn't have them.\n同时体验正面和负面情绪是人之常情。我们也许会用“负面”一词来形容那些更难受的情绪，\n但这并不意味着这些情绪是坏的、或我们不该有它们。\n【信号】转折 but 之后的纠偏表态。答案落在转折词之后：but it doesn't mean those emotions\nare bad or we shouldn't have them，说话人先承认“负面”这个叫法，转折后立刻纠正“不\n代表它坏、不代表不该有”，这句纠偏表态才是对负面情绪的真正定性。\n【替换】it doesn't mean those emotions are bad or we shouldn't have them ↔\nThey are necessary in our lives.\n双重否定转正：不该没有=有必要\nNegative emotions warn us of threats or challenges that we may need to deal with ↔\nThey are necessary in our lives.\n具体功能上升为总体定性\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey should be done away with. 的“该废除”正好是录音 we shouldn't have them（不该\n没有它们）的反面，方向相反；说话人是为负面情绪正名，A 取了相反极性。\n借⽤原⽂：it doesn't mean those emotions are bad or we shouldn't have them\nC) 未提及\nThey enrich our experience. 说负面情绪“丰富我们的体验”，听上去正面、像对的，但录音\n对负面情绪的说法是“预警、聚焦问题”，从未说它 enrich our experience，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are harmful to health. 把负面情绪定性为“有害健康”，而录音明确 it doesn't mean\nthose emotions are bad（不是坏的），还讲它有预警作用；D 取的是被说话人否定掉的\n负面定性。\n借⽤原⽂：it doesn't mean those emotions are bad", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What happens to people whose negative emotions are out of balance?", "options": ["A) They feel stressed out even without any challenges in life.", "B) They feel too overwhelmed to deal with life's problems.", "C) They are anxious to free themselves from life's troubles.", "D) They are exhausted even without doing any heavy work."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They feel too overwhelmed to deal with life's problems.\n【定位】But too many negative emotions can make us feel overwhelmed, anxious, exhausted or\nstressed out. When negative emotions are out of balance, problems might seem too big\nto handle.\n但是过多的负面情绪会让我们感到不堪重负、焦虑、疲惫或压力山大。当负面情绪失衡时，\n问题可能显得大到无法应付。\n【信号】条件词 When 直接复现题干前提。答案句以 When negative emotions are out of\nbalance 开头，与题干前提一字不差对应，紧随其后的 problems might seem too big to\nhandle 就是该条件下的结果，定位信号极明确。\n【替换】problems might seem too big to handle ↔\nThey feel too overwhelmed to deal with life's problems.\n视角转换：问题太大=人招架不住\ntoo many negative emotions can make us feel overwhelmed ↔ too overwhelmed\n原文 overwhelmed 直接对应选项 overwhelmed\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nstressed out 盗用了录音里真实出现的 stressed out，听到会想选；但 A 加的前提 even\nwithout any challenges in life（连挑战都没有也会）录音从未说，录音讲的是负面情绪“过\n多 / 失衡”才如此，前提被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：overwhelmed, anxious, exhausted or stressed out\nC) 偷换概念\nThey are anxious to free themselves from life's troubles. 说人“急于摆脱生活烦恼”，录\n音里 anxious 是“焦虑”的感受 anxious，并非“急切想做某事”，且“摆脱烦恼”这层意思录音\n根本没讲，属偷换概念加无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：overwhelmed, anxious, exhausted or stressed out\nD) 原词陷阱\nexhausted 也盗用了录音的 exhausted，但 D 的前提 even without doing any heavy\nwork（没干重活也累）录音没说，录音的因是负面情绪失衡而非体力活，前提被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：overwhelmed, anxious, exhausted or stressed out", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "How do positive emotions affect us?", "options": ["A) They expand our mind.", "B) They prolong our lives.", "C) They narrow our focus.", "D) They lessen our burdens."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They expand our mind.\n【定位】Instead of narrowing our focus like negative emotions do, positive emotions affect our brains\nin ways that increase our awareness, attention and memory.\n正面情绪不像负面情绪那样收窄我们的注意力，而是以增进我们的觉察力、注意力和记忆力\n的方式影响大脑。\n【信号】对比信号 Instead of 划清正负作用。答案句以 Instead of narrowing our focus like\nnegative emotions do 开头，用对比把“收窄”归给负面情绪，再说正面情绪 increase our\nawareness, attention and memory，对比信号直接点出正面情绪的作用方向。\n【替换】increase our awareness, attention and memory ↔ They expand our mind.\n增进多项心智能力=拓宽心智\nhelp us take in more information, hold several ideas in mind at once ↔ They expand our mind.\n容纳更多信息与想法=心智被拓宽\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nThey prolong our lives. 说正面情绪“延长寿命”，录音在此处只说它增进心智、并提到使人\nhealthier（更健康），但从未说“延长寿命”，把“更健康”拔高成“延寿”属过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：happier, healthier, learn better, and get along well with others\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey narrow our focus. 盗用了录音原词 narrowing our focus，听到会很想选；但录音用\nInstead of 明确这是“负面情绪”做的事，正面情绪恰恰相反，C 把负面情绪的作用安到了正\n面情绪头上。\n借⽤原⽂：Instead of narrowing our focus like negative emotions do\nD) 未提及\nThey lessen our burdens. 说正面情绪“减轻负担”，录音讲的是增进心智、容纳更多信息、\n表现更好，从未提到 lessen burdens，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the skirt she is wearing now?", "options": ["A) It is not easily breakable.", "B) It came from a 3D printer.", "C) It represents the latest style.", "D) It was made by a fashion designer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It came from a 3D printer.\n【定位】I had access to 3D printers. So I quickly designed a skirt on my computer, and I loaded the\nfile on the printer. It just printed the pieces overnight... this is actually the skirt that I'm\nwearing right now.\n我当时能用到 3D 打印机。于是我很快在电脑上设计了一条裙子，把文件传到打印机上。它\n连夜就把各个部件打印了出来……这就是我现在身上穿的这条裙子。\n【信号】原物回指 the skirt…right now 锁定答案句。答案句靠\"题干指的那件物\"在原文的回指来\n锁定：原文末句 this is actually the skirt that I'm wearing right now 与题干 the skirt\nshe is wearing now 完全是同一件，往前看就知道它由 3D printers 打印而来。\n【替换】It just printed the pieces overnight ↔ It came from a 3D printer.\n\"打印机连夜打印\"=\"来自 3D 打印机\"\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nnot easily breakable 借用了原文后面第4段讲\"硬塑料很易碎\"的 breakable 这一概念，但\n那是说纽约时装屋那两条裙子的材料问题，不是说她现在穿的这条裙子；把别处的属性安到\n本物上，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：they were very breakable\nC) 未提及\nrepresents the latest style 说这条裙子代表最新潮流，原文对这条裙子只讲了\"3D 打印、\n连夜打印、组装\"，从没说它是\"最新风格\"，属于无中生有。\nD) 答非所问\nmade by a fashion designer 利用了原文 I quickly designed a skirt\"我设计\"这一动作来\n诱选，但题干问的是裙子\"怎么来的\"，答案落在\"3D 打印机打印\"这一制造方式上；说\"由时\n装设计师做的\"绕开了\"打印\"这个关键，答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：I quickly designed a skirt on my computer", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "When did the speaker start experimenting with 3D printing?", "options": ["A) When she had just graduated from her college.", "B) When she attended a conference in New York.", "C) When she was studying at a fashion design school.", "D) When she attended a fashion show nine months ago."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When she was studying at a fashion design school.\n【定位】So it wasn't the first time that I printed clothes. For my senior collection at fashion design\nschool, I decided to try and 3D print an entire fashion collection from my home.\n这并不是我第一次打印衣服。在时装设计学校做毕业作品集时，我就决定尝试在家里用 3D\n打印整套时装系列。\n【信号】\"不是第一次\"+\"在设计学校\"回溯起点。答案信号是\"回溯起点\"的表述：it wasn't the first\ntime that I printed clothes 提示\"更早就做过\"，紧接着 For my senior collection at\nfashion design school 给出那段更早的时间——还在读设计学校做毕业作品时，这就是\"开\n始\"的节点。\n【替换】For my senior collection at fashion design school ↔\nWhen she was studying at a fashion design school.\n\"做毕业作品集时\"=\"在设计学校读书时\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\njust graduated from her college 说是\"刚大学毕业时\"，但原文 For my senior collection\nat fashion design school 明确是\"还在设计学校做毕业作品\"，是在校期间而非毕业之后，\n时间错位且原文也没说 college 毕业，属未提及 / 与原文不符。\nB) 张冠李戴\nattended a conference in New York 把原文两处信息拼错：第1段她确实在 technology\nconference，第4段去过 New York，但那分别是\"现在做这条裙子\"和\"实习\"的事，都不\n是\"开始尝试 3D 打印\"的节点；选项盗用 conference 与 New York 这两个原词来骗\"听到就\n选\"。\n借⽤原⽂：I was lucky to be at the technology conference then\nD) 原词陷阱\nnine months ago 借用原文 I had only nine months 这个数字，但原文 nine months 是\n说\"只有九个月时间去搞懂怎么打印\"，是\"时长\"不是\"九个月前参加时装秀\"这个时间点，更\n没有\"fashion show\"一说，典型的数字原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：I had only nine months to figure out how to print five fashionable looks", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What was the problem with the material the speaker worked on at a New York fashion house?", "options": ["A) It was difficult to print.", "B) It was hard to come by.", "C) It was hard and breakable.", "D) It was extremely expensive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It was hard and breakable.\n【定位】But I had a few problems with them. They were made from hard plastics and that's why they\nwere very breakable.\n但我对它们有些不满。它们是用硬塑料做的，所以非常容易碎。\n【信号】转折 But+problems+that's why 三重信号。答案信号叠了三层：转折词 But 提示真意要\n来，I had a few problems with them 直接点\"问题\"，再由因果 that's why 引出具体缺点\n——made from hard plastics 所以 very breakable，这句就是答案句。\n【替换】They were made from hard plastics and that's why they were very breakable. ↔\nIt was hard and breakable.\n\"硬塑料所以易碎\"=\"又硬又易碎\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\ndifficult to print 说材料难打印，但原文讲这两条裙子的问题只在\"硬、易碎、刮伤模特\"，\n从没说\"难打印\"，无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nhard to come by\"难获得\"，原文对这材料的抱怨是\"硬 / 易碎\"，从没说它难弄到手；且\nhard 一词与原文 hard plastics 的 hard 形近，易诱人误选。\n借⽤原⽂：They were made from hard plastics\nD) 未提及\nextremely expensive 说材料太贵，但原文对这两条裙子材料只字未提价格，谈的是物理性\n质（硬、碎），属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Filaflex?", "options": ["A) It is the latest model of a 3D printer.", "B) It is a plastic widely used in 3D printing.", "C) It gives fashion designers room for imagination.", "D) It marks a breakthrough in printing material."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It marks a breakthrough in printing material.\n【定位】The breakthrough came when I was introduced to Filaflex, which is a new kind of printing\nmaterial. It's strong, yet very flexible.\n当我接触到 Filaflex 时，突破就来了——它是一种新型的打印材料，既结实又非常有弹性。\n【信号】强评价词 breakthrough 标记高光落点。答案信号是强评价词 The breakthrough came\n——\"突破\"点出这是全文转好的高光落点，紧接着用 a new kind of printing material 给\nFilaflex 定性，这句即答案句。\n【替换】The breakthrough came when I was introduced to Filaflex, which is a new kind of printing material\n↔ It marks a breakthrough in printing material.\n\"突破 + 新型打印材料\"=\"打印材料的突破\"\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nthe latest model of a 3D printer 把 Filaflex 说成\"打印机的最新型号\"，但原文明确它是 a\nnew kind of printing material\"打印材料\"，材料≠打印机，把\"材料\"偷换成\"机器\"。\n借⽤原⽂：which is a new kind of printing material\nB) 与原文相反\na plastic widely used in 3D printing 借原文\"打印材料\"概念，但原文说 Filaflex 是 a new\nkind of printing material\"新型\"且 strong, yet very flexible，从没说它是\"被广泛使用的塑\n料\"——\"新型\"与\"广泛使用\"恰恰相反，且第4段的 hard plastics 才是塑料、易碎，与\nFilaflex 不是一回事，属偷换 + 与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：which is a new kind of printing material\nC) 过度推断\ngives fashion designers room for imagination 把 Filaflex 的意义拔高成\"给设计师想象空\n间\"，原文只说它 strong, yet very flexible 且能改字定制，没有上升到\"想象空间\"这一层，\n是凭可改字一步跨到抽象升华的过度推断。\n借⽤原⽂：It's strong, yet very flexible", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the problems facing small and medium sized enterprises?", "options": ["A) They arise from the advances in technology.", "B) They have not been examined in detail so far.", "C) They are easy to solve with modern technology.", "D) They can't be solved without government support."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They arise from the advances in technology.\n【定位】The purpose of today's lecture, as you have seen from the title and the abstract, is to examine\nin more detail the problems facing small- and medium-sized enterprises which arise at least in\npart from having to adapt to rapid advances in technology.\n今天讲座的目的，正如你们从标题和摘要中看到的，是更细致地审视中小企业所面临的问\n题，而这些问题至少部分源于不得不去适应技术的飞速进步。\n【信号】定语从句 which arise from 给出问题来源。答案信号是定性来源的关系从句 which arise\nat least in part from having to adapt to rapid advances in technology——开篇就交代\n“问题源于适应技术进步”，这正是对问题的定性，锁定它即可。\n【替换】arise at least in part from having to adapt to rapid advances in technology ↔\nThey arise from the advances in technology.\n概括化改写：删修饰、留主干\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说讲座目的是 to examine in more detail the problems（更细致地审视这些问题），\n选项 They have not been examined in detail so far. 盗用了 examine in detail 几个原\n词，却把意思反过来说成“至今未被仔细审视”，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：to examine in more detail the problems facing small- and medium-sized enterprises\nC) 未提及\nThey are easy to solve with modern technology. 在原文中找不到依据，录音整体把这些\n问题描述为 particularly challenging issues for small- and medium-sized enterprises\n（对中小企业尤其棘手），与“容易解决”正好相反，属无中生有兼反向。\nD) 未提及\nThey can't be solved without government support. 里的 government support 录音通\n篇未提，说话人给的解决思路是创新思维与企业间分摊成本，而非依赖政府，属凭空捏造的\n条件。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the technology industry?", "options": ["A) It is attractive to entrepreneurs.", "B) It demands huge investment.", "C) It focuses on new products.", "D) It is intensely competitive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is intensely competitive.\n【定位】Now we all know that the technology industry is intensely competitive with new products\nbeing launched all year round as the various companies strive to compete with each other,\nrather than, say, once a year or every couple of years.\n我们都知道，技术行业竞争极其激烈，新产品全年不断推出，各家公司彼此争相竞争，而不\n是说一年一次或每隔几年才出新品。\n【信号】对行业的直接概括 is intensely competitive。答案信号是 the technology industry is\nintensely competitive 这句对行业的正面概括判断，主系表结构直接给出行业属性，是典\n型的答案落点。\n【替换】the technology industry is intensely competitive ↔ It is intensely competitive.\n近乎原句，仅用 It 替换主语\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is attractive to entrepreneurs. 录音从未提及 entrepreneurs，也没说行业“吸引人”，是\n凭空附加的属性。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt demands huge investment. 张冠李戴：录音确实谈到成本高（this can be hugely\nexpensive），但那是说小公司跟进技术的花费高，主语是小企业的处境，而非把“需要巨额\n投资”当成 technology industry 本身的属性来回答本题。\n借⽤原⽂：keeping up to date with the latest technology, even if it's only for the benefit of key staff\nthis can be hugely expensive\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt focuses on new products. 偷用了录音原词 new products，但原文是说“新产品全年不\n断推出”以体现竞争激烈，并非说行业“专注于新产品”，是听到 new products 就想选的原词\n陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：with new products being launched all year round as the various companies strive to\ncompete with each other", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What is a practical solution to the problems of small and medium-sized businesses?", "options": ["A) Cooperation with big companies.", "B) Recruiting more qualified staff.", "C) In-service training of IT personnel.", "D) Sharing of costs with each other."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Sharing of costs with each other.\n【定位】Well, there's a possibility that small groups of companies with similar requirements but not\ndirectly competing with each other—they could share the cost of upgrading in much the same\nway as, let's say, an intranet operates within large organizations. In fact, cost-sharing could be\na very practical solution, especially in times of financial difficulty.\n嗯，有一种可能是：需求相似但彼此并不直接竞争的小公司组成小团体——它们可以分摊升\n级的成本，就像内联网在大型机构内部运作那样。事实上，分摊成本可以是一个非常实用的\n解决方案，尤其是在财务困难时期。\n【信号】可行性措辞 practical solution / could。答案信号是建议加可行性判断 cost-sharing\ncould be a very practical solution，practical solution 与题干 practical solution 直接呼\n应，could 表方案提议，锁定此句。\n【替换】they could share the cost of upgrading ↔ Sharing of costs with each other.\n动词短语名词化\ncost-sharing could be a very practical solution ↔ Sharing of costs with each other.\n原文 cost-sharing 即选项 sharing of costs\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nCooperation with big companies. 盗用了原文 large organizations 这个原词制造干扰，\n但原文 large organizations 只是用大机构的内联网做类比，方案的合作对象是彼此需求相\n似的小公司，不是和大公司合作，对象被偷换。\n借⽤原⽂：in much the same way as, let's say, an intranet operates within large organizations\nB) 张冠李戴\nRecruiting more qualified staff. 张冠李戴：录音前面把 recruiting high quality staff 列\n为小企业面临的问题之一，而本题问的是解决方案；把“招优质员工”这个问题误当成办法，\n答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：recruiting high quality staff in a time of skills shortages in IT\nC) 原词陷阱\nIn-service training of IT personnel. 拼用了录音里的 IT 与 trained 等熟词，但原文只把\n“留住已招募培训的员工”当问题提及，从未把“在职培训 IT 人员”作为解决方案提出，属无中\n生有的方案。\n借⽤原⽂：the issue of retaining staff once they've been recruited and trained", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) hypotheses\n名词\n【词性槽】depends on ___, experiments and reasoned methodologies——三项并列的第一项\n【依据】hypotheses 与 experiments、methodologies 同属\"科研三件套\"\nGood, sound science depends on __(26)__, experiments and reasoned\nmethodologies. 谈\"好科学的根基\"，hypotheses（假说）+ experiments（实验）+\nmethodologies（方法论）正是科研标准三步：先提假说、再做实验、再用合理方法。与下\n文 It requires a willingness to ask new questions and try new approaches. \"提新问\n题、试新方法\"完全呼应。\n【竞争词】L) inertia 惯性/惰性义，与\"好科学的基础\"完全相反\ninertia 是负面词，意为\"惰性、不愿改变\"，若代入则成\"好科学依赖惰性\"，与下文 try new\napproaches 鼓励创新的基调直接冲突，感情色彩相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·depends on ___, expe"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) contextual\n形容词\n【词性槽】requires ___ understanding——动词 + 空 + 名词，空作 understanding 的定语\n【依据】contextual understanding = 情境/背景化的理解，是科学传播的标准要素之一\nBut good science also requires __(27)__ understanding, clear explanation and\nconcise presentation. 强调好科学还需要\"理解 + 清楚解释 + 简洁呈现\"三件；contextual\nunderstanding 强调\"结合背景去理解\"，正是科学传播被广泛讨论的能力；并列三项色彩\n一致。\n【竞争词】J) indefinite 义\"模糊的、无限期的\"，与\"科学严谨\"完全相反\nindefinite understanding 表\"模糊不清的理解\"，与本段倡导的\"好科学\"立场冲突；且与 clear\nexplanation、concise presentation 这两个褒义表达色彩相反，并列不协调。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— indefinite 是形容词，词性也对，但色彩与并列项相反。\nrequires __(27)__ understanding, clear explanation and concise presentation. 三项并\n列要求同向褒义，indefinite\"模糊不清\"与 clear/concise 直接冲突。防坑：三项并列形容词\n题，第一动作不是查词义，而是查色彩是否与已给项一致；不一致直接淘汰，再在剩下的同\n向词里辨义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·requires ___ underst"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) arena\n名词\n【词性槽】the public ___ 后接介词 and offer——空前是定冠词 + public，空后跟着并列动词\noffer，本空只能是名词\n【依据】the public arena = 公共领域/舞台，是固定搭配，贴合\"走出来面向公众发声\"\nscientists who are willing to step out in the public __(28)__ and offer their opinions\non important matters 强调科学家\"走出去\"对重要议题发声，public arena \"公共舞台/公\n共领域\" 正是固定表达；与下文\"explain what they are doing in language ...\nunderstandable to the public\"形成呼应。\n【竞争词】E) digits 数字义，与\"公共发声\"语境完全无关\ndigits 指\"数字、位数\"，更适合后文 By 2008, the figure had fallen into the single __(33)__.\n谈百分比下降的语境，放在 the public ___ 里完全说不通。\nG) hypotheses 假说义，是科学家工作的对象而不是公共平台\nhypotheses 是科学研究的\"假说\"，更应出现在第一段 Good, sound science depends on\n__(26)__, experiments and reasoned methodologies. 谈科研根基的位置；the public\nhypotheses 既不成搭配，也偏离\"出面发声\"语义。\nL) inertia 惰性/惯性义，感情色彩与全段呼吁恰好相反\ninertia 表\"惰性、不愿改变\"，本段是\"鼓励科学家主动走向公众\"，若填 inertia 则成\"走进公共惰\n性\"，逻辑指向反、且 the public inertia 不是惯用搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the public ___ 后接介词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) convincing\n形容词\n【词性槽】is ___ and understandable——系动词 is 后并列两个表语形容词\n【依据】convincing 与 understandable 并列，构成\"有说服力且易懂\"，呼应\"科学家要用公众能\n接受的语言\"\nscientists who can explain what they are doing in language that is __(29)__ and\nunderstandable to the public. 强调科学家解释工作要面向公众，convincing（令人信服\n的）与 understandable（易懂的）正是科学传播两大要求；与第一段 But good science\nalso requires __(27)__ understanding, clear explanation and concise presentation.\n中\"清楚的解释、简洁的呈现\"互相呼应。\n【竞争词】B) contextual 义\"语境/上下文的\"，描述 language 时不自然\ncontextual 多用作 contextual understanding/analysis，描述\"理解/分析\"的属性；用来修饰\nlanguage 说\"语境化的语言\"既不地道，也与\"让公众听懂\"的目的不直接相关。它更贴合 27 题前\n文 But good science also requires __(27)__ understanding 那一空。\nJ) indefinite 义\"不确定的、无限期的\"，与\"易懂\"并列时方向相反\nindefinite language 意为\"模糊不清的语言\"，与 understandable 并列就成\"既模糊又易懂\"，\n自相矛盾；显然不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·is ___ and understan"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) incorporate\n动词\n【词性槽】and to ___ scientific knowledge into ...——并列 to 不定式，空是动词原形\n【依据】incorporate sth. into sth. = 把……纳入到……，是与 into 严格匹配的固定搭配\nand to __(30)__ scientific knowledge into our public communications. 表达\"把科学知\n识纳入到我们的公共交流中\"；incorporate ... into ... 是英语母语者写这一意思时最高频的\n搭配，介词 into 直接喊出了答案。\n【竞争词】K) indulge 搭配 in 而非 into；感情色彩负面\nindulge oneself in sth. 是\"沉溺\"，介词是 in 不是 into；语义\"沉溺科学知识于公共交流\"完全说\n不通。\nO) warrant 义\"使……有必要\"，不接 into 框架\nwarrant 是\"使有必要、值得\"，不与 X into Y 框架共现；语义\"保证科学知识到公共交流中\"也不\n通顺。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and to ___ scientifi"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) devoted\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the United States 后缺谓语，时间状语 In the 1960s 决定一般过去时\n【依据】devote X to Y = 把 X 投入到 Y，且 devoted 已是过去式，时态契合\nIn the 1960s, the United States __(31)__ nearly 17% of discretionary(可酌情支配的)\nspending to research and development 说\"60 年代美国把近 17% 的可支配支出投入研\n发\"；devote sth. to sth. 是固定搭配，devoted 形式直接满足 In the 1960s 要求的过去\n时；与下一句 By 2008, the figure had fallen into the single __(33)__. 形成\"过去高—后\n来跌\"的对比链条。\n【竞争词】F) hasten 义\"加快\"，且不与\"17% 支出 to 研发\"搭配\nhasten 一般接动作（hasten the process），不是\"加快 17% 到研发\"；语义和搭配都不通。\nI) incorporate 搭配是 into 不是 to\nincorporate A into B 才是地道搭配，本句介词是 to，不匹配；且语义\"把 17% 并入研发\"不\n如\"投入\"自然。\nK) indulge 义\"沉溺/纵容\"，与国家拨款语境感情色彩冲突\nindulge in 表负面\"沉溺\"，谈国家投入研发用 indulge 听起来像\"放任挥霍\"，与文段赞美\"长远\n研发投入\"的立场相反。\nO) warrant 义\"使……有必要/保证\"，与\"分配支出\"动作不对应\nwarrant 表\"使有必要、值得\"，不表\"分配\"；warrant 17% to research 也不是英语固定表达。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 the United States"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) reaping\n动词\n【词性槽】句末逗号后接 ___ decades of economic growth——独立分词作状语\n【依据】reap ... economic growth = 收获经济增长，与\"投入研发\"形成正向因果\nthe United States __(31)__ nearly 17% of discretionary(可酌情支配的) spending to\nresearch and development, __(32)__ decades of economic growth. 表达\"美国把大量支\n出投入研发，从而收获了数十年的经济增长\"；reap ... growth 是常见正向搭配（reap the\nrewards/benefits/growth），逻辑顺、感情色彩正。\n【竞争词】H) impairing 义\"损害\"，与上文\"加大研发投入\"的逻辑方向完全相反\nimpair 表\"损害\"，若代入则成\"投入研发，损害了几十年的经济增长\"，与文段\"长远研发投入很\n重要\"的立场冲突；六级常用这种\"感情色彩反向词\"来设陷阱，要警惕。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— impairing 与 reaping 同为 -ing 分词，词性完全对得上。\ndevoted nearly 17% of discretionary(可酌情支配的) spending to research and\ndevelopment, __(32)__ decades of economic growth. 这里只看语法，H 和 N 都合法；\n但 H 是\"损害\"、N 是\"收获\"，方向相反。防坑：分词作结果状语，逻辑必须沿主句方向走\n——主句在赞美\"加大研发投入\"，结果只能是正向\"收获经济增长\"，立即排除 impairing。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句末逗号后接 ___ decades o"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) digits\n名词\n【词性槽】the single ___ 句末，single 作前置定语，本空是中心名词\n【依据】single digits = 个位数，是英语谈百分比/数字下降的固定表达\n上文 nearly 17% 已给出基准比例，By 2008, the figure had fallen into the single\n__(33)__. 说\"到 2008 年这一数字跌入 single digits\"，恰好与\"17% → 个位数\"形成数值对\n比；fall into the single digits 是固定表达。\n【竞争词】G) hypotheses 义指\"假说\"，与百分比下降毫无关系\nhypotheses 谈科研假设，搭配 single 也不成立——the single hypotheses 既不像固定表达，\n也无法解释 fallen into 的\"下降\"动作。\nL) inertia 惯性/惰性，无法被\"single\"修饰\ninertia 是抽象概念，没有 the single inertia 这种搭配；而且本句要的是\"数值\"信息（与 17%\n呼应），inertia 给不出。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the single ___ 句末，si"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) pride\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 we 后缺谓语，且后面跟反身代词 ourselves——\"pride oneself on\"句型\n【依据】pride oneself on sth. = 以……自豪，是固定句型\nAt the University of California (UC), we __(34)__ ourselves not only on the quality of\nour research, but also on its contribution to improving our world. 强调 UC 对自身研\n究质量和对世界的贡献\"引以为豪\"；pride oneself on 是固定搭配，与后面 not only ... but\nalso on ... 完美咬合。\n【竞争词】F) hasten 义\"加快\"，无 hasten oneself on 的搭配\nhasten 后接动作或事物，没有 hasten oneself on sth. 这种用法；且\"使自己加快研究质量\"语\n义不通。\nI) incorporate 义\"纳入\"，搭配是 incorporate sth. into sth.\nincorporate 的固定搭配是 incorporate A into B（把 A 纳入 B），不接反身代词加 on；将其\n代入则\"把自己纳入研究质量上\"完全说不通。\nK) indulge 义\"沉溺/纵容\"，搭配是 indulge oneself in，不是 on\nindulge oneself in sth. 表\"沉溺于……\"，介词是 in 不是 on；且\"沉溺于研究质量\"感情色彩偏负\n面，与本段对 UC 的赞美口吻矛盾。\nO) warrant 义\"使……有必要/保证\"，不与反身代词搭配\nwarrant 后常接事物宾语（warrant attention/further study），不是 warrant oneself on；语\n义\"使自己有必要在质量上\"也不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 we 后缺谓语，且后面跟反身代词"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) arena", "B) contextual", "C) convincing", "D) devoted", "E) digits", "F) hasten", "G) hypotheses", "H) impairing", "I) incorporate", "J) indefinite", "K) indulge", "L) inertia", "M) pride", "N) reaping", "O) warrant"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) hasten\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to ___ the development——to 后接动词原形\n【依据】hasten the development = 加快……发展，与\"投入资金推动落地\"完全咬合\nTo __(35)__ the development of science from the lab bench to the market place, UC\nis investing our own money in our own good ideas. 是说\"为了加快科学从实验室走向市\n场，UC 拿出自己的钱投入好点子\"；hasten 表\"加速\"，与\"from the lab bench to the\nmarket place\"（从实验室到市场）的进程感、与下半句的\"投入推动\"动作完美对应。\n【竞争词】I) incorporate 义\"纳入\"，搭配 into 而非 from ... to ...\nincorporate 强调\"并入/整合\"，常与 into 连用；本句结构是 from ... to ...（从 X 到 Y），是\"加\n速过程\"的动词更搭，incorporate 与目的状语\"为了纳入科学发展……\"也讲不通。本空\nincorporate 的真正归属在 30 题。\nK) indulge 义\"沉溺/纵容\"，感情色彩与\"推动落地\"相反\nindulge 表负面\"放纵\"，谈\"为了纵容科学发展\"完全不合本段对 UC 投资行为的赞许口吻。\nO) warrant 义\"使有必要、保证\"，不表\"加速过程\"\nwarrant 表\"使……有必要、值得\"，目的状语\"为了使科学发展从实验室到市场\"语义模糊，且与下\n半句\"投钱推动\"的动作链不对应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定式 to ___ the devel"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Consumers are often hesitant to try smart-home devices because they are worried about compatibility problems.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】消费者+智能家居+兼容性问题\n【定位】Companies are pushing it hard but make it almost overwhelming even to dip a toe in\nthe water for the average consumer, because there are so many compatibility issues to\nthink about.\n公司在大力推销智能家居，但即便只是稍微试一试，对普通消费者来说也几乎不堪重负，因\n为要考虑的兼容性问题实在太多。\n【改写】Consumers are often hesitant to try smart-home devices ↔\nmake it almost overwhelming even to dip a toe in the water for the average consumer\n犹豫尝试对应几乎不堪重负去试一下\nbecause they are worried about compatibility problems ↔\nbecause there are so many compatibility issues to think about\n兼容问题原样复现\n【辨邻】 E Companies are promoting their own standards 与 Companies that have long\nfocused on hardware now have to think of ecosystems 都是从公司角度谈「推标\n准、转生态」，主语是公司，没有写消费者犹豫，因此不是题干的源头段。\nCompanies are promoting their own standards, and the market has not had time to choose a winner\nyet as this is still very new", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "This year's electronics show featured the presence of many officials from the federal government.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】今年展会+联邦政府官员到场\n【定位】In addition to many senior officials from the Federal Trade and Federal Communications\ncommissions, this year's list of policy makers also includes appearances from Transportation\nSecretary Anthony Foxx, to talk about smart cities, and Federal Aviation Administration\nAdministrator Michael Huerta, to talk about drones.\n除了联邦贸易委员会和联邦通信委员会的多位高级官员，今年的政策制定者名单上还包括交\n通部长 Anthony Foxx（谈智慧城市）和联邦航空局局长 Michael Huerta（谈无人机）。\n【改写】This year's electronics show ↔ this year's show\n近乎逐字对应\nmany officials from the federal government ↔\nmany senior officials from the Federal Trade and Federal Communications commissions\n概括上位对应具体列举", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The market demand for electronic devices is now either declining or not growing as fast as before.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】电子设备市场需求下滑或增速放缓\n【定位】But now these segments are looking at slower growth curves—or shrinking markets in some\ncases—as consumers are not as eager to spend money on new gadgets.\n但如今这些品类增速曲线变缓——有的甚至在萎缩——因为消费者不再像过去那样热衷于花\n钱买新设备。\n【改写】The market demand for electronic devices ↔\nsmartphones, televisions, tablets, laptops and desktops have made up a huge part of the\nmarket\n电子设备对应智能手机电视平板等的并列\nis now either declining or not growing as fast as before ↔\nthese segments are looking at slower growth curves—or shrinking markets in some cases\n增速放缓或萎缩的并列同义\n【辨邻】 G only 48 percent said yes—a six-point drop from 2015 只覆盖「今年想买新手机的\n人比例」这一单点数据，没有像题干那样把电子设备整体打包说成「下滑或放缓」，而\n是消费者意愿调查的局部结论。\nwhen asked whether they would buy a new smartphone this year, only 48 percent said yes—a six-\npoint drop from 2015", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "One analyst suggests it is necessary to accept both the positive and negative aspects of innovative products.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一位分析师主张同时接受正反两面\n【定位】We have to understand and think about the implications, and balance these great innovations\nwith the potential downsides they naturally carry with them.\n我们必须理解并思考这些影响，在这些伟大的创新与它们天然伴随的潜在负面之间取得\n平衡。\n【改写】One analyst ↔ Curran, the Accenture analyst\n一位分析师对应Accenture分析师\nit is necessary to accept both the positive and negative aspects of innovative products ↔\nbalance these great innovations with the potential downsides they naturally carry with them\n接受正反对应在创新与负面之间求平衡\n【辨邻】 F John Curran, managing director of research at Accenture 的发言聚焦于 we are\nstarting to see companies shift from what is the largest screen size, the\nsmallest form factor or the shiniest object and more into what all of these\ndevices do that is practical，讲的是「从炫酷转向务实」，与「接受创新的正反两\n面」不是同一观点。\nwe are starting to see companies shift from what is the largest screen size, the smallest form factor\nor the shiniest object and more into what all of these devices do that is practical in a consumer's life", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The Consumer Electronics Show in recent years has begun to focus more on the practical value than the showiness of electronic devices.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近几年CES更看重实用价值而非炫酷\n【定位】But over the last couple of years, and in this one in particular, we are starting to see\ncompanies shift from what is the largest screen size, the smallest form factor or the shiniest\nobject and more into what all of these devices do that is practical in a consumer's life.\n但在过去几年里、尤其是今年，我们开始看到公司不再比拼最大的屏幕、最小的外形或最炫\n的造型，而是更关注这些设备在消费者生活中究竟有什么实际用处。\n【改写】The Consumer Electronics Show in recent years ↔\nover the last couple of years, and in this one in particular\n近几年对应过去几年尤其今年\nfocus more on the practical value than the showiness of electronic devices ↔\nshift from what is the largest screen size, the smallest form factor or the shiniest object and\nmore into what all of these devices do that is practical in a consumer's life\n更看重实用而非炫酷对应从炫酷属性转向实用价值\n【辨邻】 A Many of the coolest gadgets this year are the same as the coolest gadgets last\nyear—or the year before, even 是「炫酷重复」的感慨，并未提出 CES 把焦点转向\n实用价值。\nMany of the coolest gadgets this year are the same as the coolest gadgets last year—or the year\nbefore, even", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Fewer innovative products were found at this year's electronic products show.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】今年展会创新产品变少\n【定位】Scan the highlights of this year's Consumer Electronics Show (CES), and you may get a\nslight feeling of having seen them before. Many of the coolest gadgets this year are the same\nas the coolest gadgets last year—or the year before, even.\n扫一眼今年消费电子展（CES）的亮点，你可能会有一种「似曾相识」的微妙感觉。今年许\n多最酷的小玩意儿，和去年的、甚至前年的，几乎都是同一批。\n【改写】Fewer innovative products were found at this year's electronic products show ↔\nMany of the coolest gadgets this year are the same as the coolest gadgets last year—or the year\nbefore, even\n创新产品更少对应今年酷玩与去年雷同\nthis year's electronic products show ↔ this year's Consumer Electronics Show (CES)\n今年的电子产品展对应今年的CES\n【辨邻】 C There is not any one-hit wonder, and there will not be one for years to come\n与 that doesn't necessarily mean that innovation has stopped 是「对创新少的\n解释」——承认无爆款但否认创新停止；不是题干那种「创新产品更少」的纯现象\n观察。\nThere is not any one-hit wonder, and there will not be one for years to come", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Consumers are becoming more worried about giving personal information to tech companies to get customized products and services.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为换定制产品而交个人信息的焦虑\n【定位】And when it comes to the hyper-connected super-smart world that technology firms are\npainting for us, it seems that consumers are growing more uneasy about handing over the\nmassive amounts of consumer data needed to provide the personalized, customized solutions\nthat companies need to improve their services.\n至于科技公司为我们描绘的那个超连接、超智能的世界，消费者似乎对交出大量个人数据这\n件事越来越不安——而正是这些数据，公司才能用来提供个性化、定制化的解决方案，进而\n改进自己的服务。\n【改写】Consumers are becoming more worried about ↔ consumers are growing more uneasy about\n更担心对应越来越不安\ngiving personal information to tech companies ↔\nhanding over the massive amounts of consumer data\n给科技公司提供个人信息对应交出大量消费者数据\nto get customized products and services ↔ to provide the personalized, customized solutions\n定制产品/服务对应个性化定制方案\n【辨邻】 J lack of enthusiasm could stem from concerns about privacy and security 与 18\npercent have even returned devices 谈的是更广义的隐私安全担忧和退货比例，没\n有把「为换定制服务而交数据」这条因果链单独点出，对应度不如 H 段精准。\nconcerns about privacy and security", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The Consumer Technology Association is the sponsor of the annual Consumer Electronics Show.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】CTA是CES的主办方\n【定位】DuBravac works for CTA—which puts on the show each year—and said that this shift to a\nsearch for solutions has been noticeable as he researched his predictions for 2016.\nDuBravac 任职于 CTA——这家机构正是每年主办这场展会的——他说，这种「转向寻找解\n决方案」的趋势在他做 2016 年预测时已经很明显。\n【改写】The Consumer Technology Association ↔ CTA\n全称对应缩写\nis the sponsor of the annual Consumer Electronics Show ↔ puts on the show each year\n主办年度展会对应每年举办这场展会\n【辨邻】 C Gary Shapiro, president and chief executive of the Consumer Technology\nAssociation (CTA) 只交代了人物在 CTA 的身份，未陈述 CTA 与 CES 之间的主办\n关系。\nGary Shapiro, president and chief executive of the Consumer Technology Association (CTA)", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Many consumers wonder about the necessity of having their fitness monitored.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对自己健身被监测的必要性产生质疑\n【定位】Yet the persistent question of Why do I need that?—or, perhaps more tellingly, Why do you\nneed to know that?—dogs the steps of many new ventures.\n然而，「我为什么需要这个？」——或更耐人寻味的「你为什么需要知道这些？」——这一\n持续不去的质问，仍然纠缠着许多新企业前行的脚步。\n【改写】Many consumers wonder about the necessity ↔ Why do I need that?\n质疑必要性对应我为什么需要这个\nhaving their fitness monitored ↔ tracking our steps and our very heartbeats\n健身被监测对应追踪步数和心跳\n【辨邻】 J concerns about privacy and security 与 safer guarantees against having their\nsensitive information hacked 谈的是隐私和安全风险，不是 wonder about the\nnecessity 的「质疑必要性」。\nlack of enthusiasm could stem from concerns about privacy and security", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "The electronic industry is maturing even though no wonder products hit the market.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无爆款但行业在成熟\n【定位】There is not any one-hit wonder, and there will not be one for years to come, said Gary\nShapiro, president and chief executive of the Consumer Technology Association (CTA). In his\neyes, however, that doesn't necessarily mean that innovation has stopped. It has just grown up\na little. Many industries are going out of infancy and becoming adolescents, Shapiro said.\n「没有任何一鸣惊人的爆款，未来几年内也不会有，」消费技术协会（CTA）总裁兼首席执\n行官 Gary Shapiro 说。然而在他看来，这并不意味着创新已经停止，它只是稍微长大了一\n点。「许多行业正从婴儿期走向青春期，」Shapiro 说道。\n【改写】The electronic industry is maturing ↔\nMany industries are going out of infancy and becoming adolescents\n成熟中对应脱离婴儿期走向青春期\nno wonder products hit the market ↔\nThere is not any one-hit wonder, and there will not be one for years to come\n无爆款上市对应没有一鸣惊人的爆款\neven though ↔ that doesn't necessarily mean that innovation has stopped\n让步关系对应虽然没爆款但创新没停在成长\n【辨邻】 B slower growth curves—or shrinking markets in some cases 描述的是市场需求指\n标的变化，而非「行业从婴儿期走向青春期」那种结构成熟。\nthese segments are looking at slower growth curves—or shrinking markets in some cases\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA\n走进今年的消费电子展（CES），亮点似乎和去年甚至前年雷同——无人机、3D打印\n机、虚拟现实眼镜、各种智能设备目不暇接，乍一看仍令人眼花缭乱，细想却觉得似\n曾相识，不禁让人怀疑：Are we in an innovation lull？这段把整篇文章的「创新是不\n是停滞了」这一发问立起来。\n信息指纹 似曾相识的CES亮点+发问创新间歇期\nB 回答上一段的发问：在某种意义上确实如此。智能手机、电视、平板、笔记本和台式\n机长期占据市场大头并驱动创新，但如今这些品类增速放缓、有的甚至在萎缩，消费\n者也不再热衷为新设备掏钱；与此同时，无人机、3D打印机、智能家居这些新兴技术\n也「老」得难再被称为下一个风口。\n信息指纹 传统电子品类增速放缓或萎缩\nC CTA 总裁兼CEO Gary Shapiro 的判断：科技业现在处在一个尴尬期，没有也不会很快\n出现「一鸣惊人的爆款」，但这并不意味着创新停止，只是「长大了一点」——许多\n行业正从婴儿期走向青春期。\n信息指纹 无爆款但行业由婴儿期走向青春期\nD 举智能家居为例：在已有技术之上的新技术之所以难普及，是因为它们必须和其它设\n备协同才能展现完整价值；公司大力推销，但兼容性问题太多，让普通消费者望而却\n步——日历软件是否和冰箱兼容、洗衣机和平板能否互通，都让人头大；每个智能家\n电都要装一个 app 也很烦人。\n信息指纹 智能家居因兼容性问题让普通人却步\nE 各家公司都在推自家的标准，市场还没来得及选出赢家；长期专注硬件的公司不得不\n转向「生态系统」思维，为消费者提供日常问题的实际解决方案。经济学家 Shawn\nDuBravac 指出：对话正从「技术上有没有可能」转向「技术上是不是有意义」。本段\n也顺便点明 DuBravac 服务于 CTA，而 CTA 正是每年举办这场展会的组织方。\n信息指纹 CTA每年举办CES+对话从可能转向有意义\nF\nAccenture 研究总监 John Curran 指出：过去 CES 主打「酷炫」——比谁屏幕大、外\n形小、东西亮；但最近几年、尤其是今年，公司开始转向「这些设备在消费者生活中\n到底有什么实际用处」。新闻发布会也由「炫技」转为「务实」，Fitbit 推出首款智\n能手表时就明确定位「工具而非玩具」，并支持 iOS、Android、Windows Phone 多\n平台。\n信息指纹 CES从炫酷转向务实\nG\n消费者本身也在变：Accenture 对 28 个国家 28000 名消费者的调查显示，大众对科技\n产品的兴奋度大不如前，比如今年只有 48% 的受访者表示会买新手机，比 2015 年下\n降了 6 个百分点。\n信息指纹 买新手机意愿下降6个百分点\nH 面对科技公司描绘的「超连接智慧世界」，消费者越来越不愿意把那些用于定制服\n务所需的大量个人数据交出去；这也部分解释了为何公司近来更强调设备的「实\n用性」。\n信息指纹 为换定制服务交个人数据消费者更不安\nI\n科技已渗入生活方方面面、记录我们的步数和心跳，但「我为什么需要这个？」「你\n为什么需要知道这些？」这类质问一直困扰新产品；只有 13% 的人表示 2016 年想买\n智能手表，比上一年只涨 1 个百分点；健身监测器、智能恒温器、联网家庭摄像头的\n需求也持平。\n信息指纹 质问自身健身/心跳被监测的必要性\nJ 调查表明，热情消退可能源于对隐私与安全的担忧：即便是已经买过联网设备的人\n里，也有 37% 说今后会更谨慎使用，18% 甚至已经退货，直到能拿到更可靠的安全\n保证才肯继续。\n信息指纹 隐私安全担忧致退货数据\nK\n这也解释了为何今年展会上有大量华盛顿官员到场——新技术正闯入受严格监管的领\n域。除了联邦贸易委员会、联邦通信委员会的多位高官，今年的政策制定者名单上还\n有交通部长 Anthony Foxx（谈智慧城市）和联邦航空局局长 Michael Huerta（谈无\n人机）。\n信息指纹 联邦官员云集CES\nL Accenture 分析师 Curran 进一步解释：随着科技在生活中分量加重，政府对展会兴趣\n上升合情合理；技术正变得更宏大、更具抱负，渗入生活的方方面面，我们必须理解\n其影响，在伟大创新与其天然伴随的潜在负面之间取得平衡。\n信息指纹 在创新与潜在负面之间求平衡", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "The author is critical of the Paris climate agreement because______.", "options": ["A) it is unfair to those climate-vulnerable nations", "B) it aims to keep temperature rise below 2℃ only", "C) it is beneficial to only fewer than 4% of countries", "D) it burdens developed countries with the sole responsibility"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 The author is critical of the Paris climate agreement because 用 because 让考生\n在原文找出作者批评的具体理由，是典型的因果细节题。\n【拆句】Q46 是一个待补全的陈述句：主句 \"The author is critical of the Paris climate\nagreement\" 后接 because 引导的原因状语从句，空格处填的是作者批评协议的原因。结\n构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】On the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change\nimpacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem.\n另一方面，存在着许多 \"被迫搭车者\"——他们几乎没有制造问题，却在承受气候变化的\n冲击。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsuffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem ↔\nunfair to those climate-vulnerable nations\nMany of the world's most climate vulnerable countries ↔ climate-vulnerable nations\n预判答案 \"协定对气候脆弱国家不公\" 与 it is unfair to those climate-vulnerable\nnations 字面一致，直接锁定。\nB) 与原文相反\nit aims to keep temperature rise below 2℃ only 把 keep global warming well\nbelow 2℃ 这一目标本身当作批评对象，但作者在 The goal of keeping global\ntemperature rise \"well below\" 2℃ is commendable 明确说这一目标是\ncommendable 值得肯定的，与批评相反。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 Fewer than 4% of countries are responsible for more than half of the world's\ngreenhouse gas emissions 说的是 \"不到 4% 的国家造成过半排放\"——是责任比例，\n而非协议受益比例；选项 beneficial to only fewer than 4% of countries 把责任偷换\n成 \"协议只让不到 4% 的国家受益\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 the agreement specifies that there is no formal distinction between\ndeveloped and developing nations in their responsibility to cut emissions 明确说\n协议没有把责任只压到发达国家头上，反而抹平了区别；选项 burdens developed\ncountries with the sole responsibility 正好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Why does the author call some developed countries climate \"free-riders\"?", "options": ["A) They needn't worry about the food and water they consume.", "B) They are better able to cope with the global climate change.", "C) They hardly pay anything for the problems they have caused.", "D) They are free from the greenhouse effects affecting \"forced riders\"."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author call some developed countries climate \"free-riders\" 用\nWhy 问作者这样称呼的依据，要的是原文对应解释句，属细节题。\n【拆句】Q47 是 why 引导的疑问句，宾补结构 \"call some developed countries climate free-\nriders\"——把发达国家 \"称为\" 气候搭便车者，问的是这样称呼的原因。结构清晰，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Developed nations such as Australia, the United States, Canada, and European countries are\nessentially climate \"free-riders\": causing the majority of the problems through high\ngreenhouse gas emissions, while incurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact\non food and water.\n诸如澳大利亚、美国、加拿大及欧洲国家这些发达国家本质上就是气候上的 \"搭便车者\"：通\n过高温室气体排放制造了绝大多数问题，却几乎不承担诸如气候变化对食物与水资源冲击之\n类的代价。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 climate change's impact on food and water 中的 food and water 是气候变化\n冲击的对象，并非他们 \"消费\" 的对象；选项 They needn't worry about the food and\nwater they consume 把 food/water 偷换成 \"自己消费的食物和水\"。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未说发达国家 better able to cope with the global climate change 应对能力更\n强——它谈的是 \"承担成本少\" 而非 \"应对能力强\"，二者不能等同。\nC) ✓ 正确\nincurring few of the costs such as climate change's impact on food and water ↔\nhardly pay anything for the problems they have caused\ncausing the majority of the problems ↔ the problems they have caused\n预判答案 \"造成问题却不承担代价\" 直接对应 hardly pay anything for the problems\nthey have caused，锁定 C。\nD) 偷换概念\n题干和原文里 free-riders 中的 free 来自 free-rider（搭便车，免责），不是 free from\n（免受影响）；选项 free from the greenhouse effects affecting \"forced riders\" 利\n用一词多义把 \"免责\" 改造成 \"免受影响\"，与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why does the author compare the \"forced riders\" to second-hand smokers?", "options": ["A) They have little responsibility for public health problems.", "B) They are vulnerable to unhealthy environmental conditions.", "C) They have to bear consequences they are not responsible for.", "D) They are unaware of the potential risks they are confronting."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author compare the \"forced riders\" to second-hand smokers 问\n作者打这个比喻是为说明什么，答案在比喻句邻近的解释/类比说明里。\n【拆句】Q48 是 Why 疑问句，主干 \"the author compare the forced riders to second-hand\nsmokers\"——\"compare A to B\" 表示把 A 比作 B；问的是这个类比想表达什么。结构无\n陷阱。\n【定位】On the flip side, there are many \"forced riders\", who are suffering from the climate change\nimpacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem. ... This is much like a non-\nsmoker getting cancer from second-hand smoke, while the heavy smoker is fortunate enough\nto smoke in good health.\n另一方面，有许多 \"被迫搭车者\"，他们几乎未制造问题，却在承受气候变化冲击……这就好\n比一个不吸烟的人因为二手烟得了癌症，而吸烟成性的人却幸运地一边抽烟一边健康如常。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\n题干问的是为何把 forced riders 比作 second-hand smokers，要的是受害与责任之间\n的对比；little responsibility for public health problems 跑到 public health（公共卫\n生）这个无关领域，没有回答比喻意图。\nB) 以偏概全\nvulnerable to unhealthy environmental conditions 只说出 \"易受不健康环境影响\" 这\n一表层结果，丢掉了比喻的核心——\"非由自己造成\"；只取一半语义，构成以偏概全。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsuffering from the climate change impacts despite having scarcely contributed to the problem ↔\nbear consequences they are not responsible for\n二手烟比喻的核心点是 \"受害者非责任方\"，They have to bear consequences they\nare not responsible for 正是这一核心的直接转述，锁定 C。\nD) 无中生有\n原文从未提 forced riders unaware of the potential risks \"不知道潜在风险\"——他们\n恰恰深受其害，对风险心知肚明；意识与否是凭空添加的命题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author say about the $100 billion funding?", "options": ["A) It will motivate all nations to reduce carbon emissions.", "B) There is no final agreement on where it will come from.", "C) There is no clarification of how the money will be spent.", "D) It will effectively reduce greenhouse emissions worldwide."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about the \\$100 billion funding 问作者就这笔钱说了什\n么具体事实，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】Q49 是 What 引导的疑问句，宾语 \"the \\$100 billion funding\" 是询问对象。结构清晰，\n注意 \\$ 是美元符号，无陷阱。\n【定位】There is also very little detail on who will provide the funds or, importantly, who is\nresponsible for their provision.\n至于由谁出资，更关键的是谁来负责筹措这些资金，协议也几乎没有任何细节。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文 supporting developing nations to reduce emissions 只说资金用于支持发展中\n国家减排，并未承诺这能 motivate all nations to reduce carbon emissions \"激励所\n有国家减排\"；从 \"支持部分国家\" 推到 \"激励所有国家\" 是过度推断。\nB) ✓ 正确\nvery little detail on who will provide the funds ↔\nno final agreement on where it will come from\n预判 \"出资方尚无定论\" 与 There is no final agreement on where it will come from\n一一对应，直接锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 who will provide the funds or... who is responsible for their provision 谈的是\n\"谁出钱\" 的来源问题，选项 no clarification of how the money will be spent 偷换成\n\"钱怎么花\" 的去向问题，方向不一致。\nD) 无中生有\neffectively reduce greenhouse emissions worldwide 在原文里找不到对应——P7 反\n而暗示协议在减排责任划分上模糊，无法效力全球减排；这是凭空添加的乐观结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What urgent action must be taken to realise the Paris climate agreement?", "options": ["A) Encouraging high-emitting nations to take the initiative.", "B) Calling on all the nations concerned to make joint efforts.", "C) Pushing the current world leaders to come to a consensus.", "D) Putting in effect the policies in the agreement at once."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What urgent action must be taken to realise the Paris climate agreement 问的是\n具体待采取的措施，原文有明确对应的祈使/必须句，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】Q50 是 What 引导的疑问句，主干 \"What urgent action must be taken\" 用被动语态问什\n么紧迫行动必须被采取；不定式 \"to realise the Paris climate agreement\" 作目的状语。\n结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】There must urgently be a meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement if\nwe are to achieve national emissions reductions while helping the most vulnerable countries\nadapt to climate change.\n如果我们想在实现各国减排的同时帮助最脆弱的国家适应气候变化，就必须紧急且切实地把\n协定所述政策动员起来落到实处。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nEncouraging high-emitting nations to take the initiative 来源是 it is clearly up to\nthe current generation of leaders from high-emitting nations to decide，那是一\n句关于 \"由谁拍板\" 的尾段呼吁，不是题干所问的 \"urgent action\"；urgent action 在原\n文中明确锁在 P8 的 must urgently 一句。\nB) 无中生有\nCalling on all the nations concerned to make joint efforts 是听起来很顺耳的常识口\n号，原文 P8 强调的是 \"把已写入协定的政策动员起来\"，并非 \"号召所有国家联合\"——\n号召联合属于无中生有。\nC) 无中生有\nPushing the current world leaders to come to a consensus 的 \"达成共识\" 来源是脑\n补——P9 谈的是高排放国领导者将被历史如何评价，不是要他们 \"达成共识\"；原文没\n有任何 consensus 相关诉求。\nD) ✓ 正确\na meaningful mobilisation of the policies outlined in the agreement ↔\nPutting in effect the policies in the agreement\nurgently ↔ at once\n预判 \"立刻落实协议政策\" 与 Putting in effect the policies in the agreement at once\n字面一致，直接锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author mean by saying \"Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon sign\" (Lines 1–2, Para. 1)?", "options": ["A) Mental problems can now be found in large numbers of teenagers.", "B) Teenagers' mental problems are getting more and more attention.", "C) Teenagers' mental problems are often too conspicuous not to be observed.", "D) Depression and anxiety are the most common symptoms of mental problems."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author mean by saying 明确要求解释一句话的真实含义，标志着这\n是一道考查整句修辞含义的句意题。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the author mean by saying...?：宾语从句省略，引号内是被动语态\nlike 引导明喻，句子结构平铺直叙，无陷阱，重心在 like a neon sign 这个比喻。\n【定位】Teenagers at risk of depression, anxiety and suicide often wear their troubles like a neon(霓\n虹灯) sign. Their risky behaviors—drinking too much alcohol, using illegal drugs, smoking\ncigarettes and skipping school—can alert parents and teachers that serious problems\nare brewing.\n面临抑郁、焦虑与自杀风险的青少年，常常把自己的烦恼像霓虹灯一样挂在身上。他们的危\n险行为——酗酒、吸毒、抽烟、逃学——能提醒家长和老师严重问题正在酝酿。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nMental problems can now be found in large numbers of teenagers 把焦点偷换为”\n患病人数多”，而原句讲的是”症状显眼”，与人数规模无关。\nB) 无中生有\nTeenagers' mental problems are getting more and more attention 谈”日益受到重\n视”，原文只字未提社会关注度的变化。\nC) ✓ 正确\nwear their troubles like a neon(霓虹灯) sign ↔ too conspicuous not to be observed\ncan alert parents and teachers ↔ not to be observed\n预判要点是”显眼+能被发现”，C 项 too conspicuous not to be observed 把两点压成\n一句，是定位句的精准同义改写。\nD) 答非所问\nDepression and anxiety are the most common symptoms 讨论”哪种症状最常见”，\n与题干所问的”霓虹灯句意”无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is the finding of the new study?", "options": ["A) Teenagers' lifestyles have changed greatly in recent years.", "B) Many teenagers resort to drugs or alcohol for mental relief.", "C) Teenagers experiencing psychological problems tend to use a lot of media.", "D) Many hitherto unobserved youngsters may have psychological problems."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding 指向一个具体的事实性结论，不是要推断也不是主旨概括。\n【拆句】What is the finding of the new study? 单主谓宾的极简疑问句，无修饰、无陷阱。\n【定位】But a new study finds that there's another group of adolescents who are in nearly as much\ndanger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms: teens who use tons of media, don't\nget enough sleep and have a sedentary(不爱活动的) lifestyle.\n但一项新研究发现，还有另一群青少年同样处于经历这些精神症状的危险中：那些大量使用\n媒体、睡眠不足、久坐少动的青少年。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTeenagers' lifestyles have changed greatly in recent years 是一个时序变化论断，原\n文仅描述一群少年的当前状态，未做时序对比。\nB) 张冠李戴\nMany teenagers resort to drugs or alcohol for mental relief 描述的是 P1 中的高风\n险组特征，且原文从未说他们靠毒品酒精”自我缓解”，而是说这些行为本身是危险征兆。\nC) 与原文相反\nTeenagers experiencing psychological problems tend to use a lot of media 把因\n果方向颠倒：原文是”高媒介使用等行为→精神风险”，C 把”已有精神问题→倾向使用媒\n体”反着说。\nD) ✓ 正确\nanother group of adolescents ↔ Many hitherto unobserved youngsters\nin nearly as much danger of experiencing the same psychiatric symptoms ↔\nmay have psychological problems\n预判两要点：①一群被忽视的少年 ②同样有精神问题风险，D 项用 hitherto\nunobserved 与 may have psychological problems 一一对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why do the researchers refer to teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary lifestyle as the \"invisible risk\" group?", "options": ["A) Their behaviors can be an invisible threat to society.", "B) Their behaviors do not constitute a warning signal.", "C) Their behaviors do not tend towards mental problems.", "D) Their behaviors can be found in almost all teenagers on earth."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why do the researchers refer to...as the \"invisible risk\" group 问的是作者为何这样称\n呼，答案就在命名句旁边的解释里。\n【拆句】宾语从句 teens who use tons of media, don't get enough sleep and have a sedentary\nlifestyle 是三个并列动词词组作 who 的定语，描述同一群青少年，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】Because their behaviors are not usually seen as a red flag, these young people have been\ndubbed the \"invisible risk\" group by the study's authors.\n因为他们的行为通常不被视为危险信号，这些少年被研究的作者们称为”隐性风险”组。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTheir behaviors can be an invisible threat to society 把”隐性”嫁接到”社会威胁”上，\n原文只谈对个人精神健康的风险，从未上升到社会危害。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnot usually seen as a red flag ↔ do not constitute a warning signal\n预判要点是”不被当作危险信号”，B 项就是 not...a red flag 的字面同义翻译。\nC) 与原文相反\nTheir behaviors do not tend towards mental problems 说他们的行为不会导致精神\n问题，而原文整段都在强调这些行为同样会引发精神症状——方向完全相反。\nD) 以偏概全\nTheir behaviors can be found in almost all teenagers on earth 借用了 every\nteenager on the planet，但原文紧接着用 But 强调”只有把三项都做到极端的少年才真\n正危险”，D 把这条限定吞掉了。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the new study find about the invisible group?", "options": ["A) They are almost as liable to depression as the high-risk group.", "B) They suffer from depression without showing any symptoms.", "C) They do not often demonstrate risky behaviors as their peers.", "D) They do not attract the media attention the high-risk group does."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the new study find about the invisible group 仍是问一个具体的客观发现，\n不需要推断。\n【拆句】What does the new study find about the invisible group? 介词 about 引出对象限定，\n单层疑问句，无陷阱。\n【定位】The group that scored high on all nine of the risk behaviors was most likely to show\nsymptoms of depression; in all, nearly 15% of this group reported being depressed, compared\nwith just 4% of the low-risk group. But the invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set,\nwith more than 13% of them exhibiting depression.\n在九种风险行为上得分都高的那一组最容易表现出抑郁症状；总体而言，这一组中近 15%\n的人报告称自己抑郁，而低风险组仅 4%。但隐性风险组并不比高风险组落后多少，超过\n13% 的人表现出抑郁。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe invisible group wasn't far behind the high-risk set ↔\nalmost as liable to depression as the high-risk group\nmore than 13% of them exhibiting depression ↔ liable to depression\n预判要点是”几乎不亚于高风险组”，A 项用 almost as...as 与 the high-risk group 把对\n比关系与对比对象一并写出。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey suffer from depression without showing any symptoms 说他们抑郁却”无症\n状”，而原文清清楚楚说 13% of them exhibiting depression，恰恰是有症状被研究观\n察到。\nC) 答非所问\nThey do not often demonstrate risky behaviors as their peers 答的是”隐性”何来，\n是 53 题层面问题，而非 54 题问的”新研究关于他们的发现”。\nD) 无中生有\nThey do not attract the media attention the high-risk group does 谈媒体注意度，\n原文从未谈及媒体对两组的报道差异。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2016-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is the significance of Vladimir Carli's study?", "options": ["A) It offers a new treatment for psychological problems among teenagers.", "B) It provides new early-warning signals for identifying teens in trouble.", "C) It may have found an ideal way to handle teenagers with behavioral problems.", "D) It sheds new light on how unhealthy behaviors trigger mental health problems."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the significance of Vladimir Carli's study 问的是研究者本人在文中陈述的研究意\n义，是明示性陈述，不必推断。\n【拆句】What is the significance of Vladimir Carli's study? 简单主系表疑问句，of 短语作定语限\n定 study，无陷阱。\n【定位】Carli says that one of the most significant things about his study is that it provides new early-\nwarning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers.\nCarli 说，他研究最重要的意义之一在于，它为家长、老师和心理保健工作者提供了新的早\n期预警信号。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIt offers a new treatment for psychological problems among teenagers 把”早期预\n警信号”升级为”新治疗方法”，原文末句虽提及 treatment，但研究本身提供的是预警而\n非治疗。\nB) ✓ 正确\nprovides new early-warning signs for parents, teachers and mental health-care providers ↔\nprovides new early-warning signals for identifying teens in trouble\n预判要点是”提供新的早期预警信号”，B 项几乎原词复现 early-warning signals，把信\n号面向的目的写出。\nC) 过度推断\nIt may have found an ideal way to handle teenagers with behavioral problems 把\n研究的意义升级为”找到了理想的处置办法”，原文只说”提供新的早期预警信号”，远没有\nideal way 这种程度。\nD) 无中生有\nIt sheds new light on how unhealthy behaviors trigger mental health problems\n谈”揭示不健康行为如何引发心理问题的机制”，原文未做任何机制层面的解释。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
